window.SHEETS={"P1": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> dimensions of solid angle (24.7),\ndimensionless combination (24.40), dimensional analysis (25.24), % error\npropagation (25.42), new unit light-time distance (26.4), significant\nfigures in density (26.21), dimensions of constants (R.38).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dimensions: G [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]; h [ML²T⁻¹]; ε₀ [M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A²]; μ₀\n[MLT⁻²A⁻²]; c² = 1/(μ₀ε₀); pressure = energy density [ML⁻¹T⁻²]; work =\ntorque = energy [ML²T⁻²]; angle and solid angle are dimensionless;\nstrain, refractive index, relative density have no unit.</li>\n<li>Error for x = aᵖbᵠ/cʳ: Δx/x = p(Δa/a) + q(Δb/b) + r(Δc/c) (worst\ncase, errors always add).</li>\n<li>Significant figures: multiplication/division keep the fewest sig\nfigs; addition/subtraction keep the fewest decimal places. Zeros between\ndigits and trailing zeros after a decimal point count.</li>\n<li>Least count (LC) of an instrument = smallest division; error in a\nsingle reading = ±LC.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> To test a formula, check that both sides have\nthe same dimensions; a dimension check cannot fix numerical constants\n(½, 2π).</li>\n</ul>\n", "P2": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> uniform circular motion acceleration\n(24.15), v–t to a–t graph (24.38), acceleration from t–x relation\n(25.15), relative velocity of buses (25.21), v–t plots for a thrown ball\n(26.5), ruler-drop reaction time (26.30), average speed (R.16),\ncircular-motion graph (R.27).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>v = u + at; s = ut + ½at²; v² = u² + 2as; distance in the nth second\nsₙ = u + a(2n − 1)/2.</li>\n<li>Average speed over equal distances: 2v₁v₂/(v₁ + v₂); over equal\ntimes: (v₁ + v₂)/2.</li>\n<li>Projectile: T = 2u sinθ/g; H = u²sin²θ/2g; R = u²sin2θ/g; R_max at\n45°; H = R tanθ/4; complementary angles (θ, 90° − θ) give the same\nrange.</li>\n<li>Relative velocity: v_AB = v_A − v_B (vectors); river-crossing\nshortest time heads straight across; shortest path needs sinθ =\nv_river/v_boat.</li>\n<li>Uniform circular motion: a = v²/r = ω²r (toward the centre); speed\nconstant, velocity changing.</li>\n<li>Ruler drop (26.30): d = ½gt², so t = √(2d/g).</li>\n<li>Graph rules: slope of x–t = velocity; slope of v–t = acceleration;\narea under v–t = displacement; area under a–t = change in velocity.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> If a v–t graph is a straight line, v–t slope\nconstant → a–t is a horizontal line.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P3": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> tension in circular motion (24.23),\ncontact force between blocks (24.26), friction on inclines (25.4),\nimpulse in a ball collision (25.10), acceleration under perpendicular\nforces (26.14), friction of a box on a trolley (26.35), banked road\n(R.26).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>F = ma = dp/dt; impulse J = FΔt = Δp (area under F–t).</li>\n<li>Friction: f_s ≤ μ_sN; f_k = μ_kN; angle of repose tanθ = μ_s; on an\nincline a = g(sinθ − μcosθ).</li>\n<li>Atwood: a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂); T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂).</li>\n<li>Blocks pushed together by force F on m₁: a = F/(m₁ + m₂); contact\nforce on m₂ = m₂F/(m₁ + m₂).</li>\n<li>Lift: apparent weight = m(g ± a) (up-accelerating +).</li>\n<li>Banking: no friction tanθ = v²/(rg); with friction v_max² = rg(μ +\ntanθ)/(1 − μtanθ). On a level road v_max = √(μrg).</li>\n<li>Net force from perpendicular forces: F = √(F₁² + F₂²).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Draw a free-body diagram first; write ΣF along\nmotion = ma for each body, then solve.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P4": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> inelastic collision velocity ratio\n(24.17), instantaneous power (24.20), work–energy for braking (25.5),\nvertical circle with slack string (25.7), crane power (26.17), elastic\ncollision then pendulum height (R.21), work by variable friction\n(R.23).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>W = F·d cosθ; W = ∫F dx = area under F–x; W_net = ΔKE; KE =\np²/2m.</li>\n<li>Power P = W/t = F·v (instantaneous).</li>\n<li>Spring PE = ½kx²; conservation of mechanical energy when only\nconservative forces act.</li>\n<li>Elastic collision (1D): v₁′ = [(m₁ − m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂]/(m₁ + m₂); equal\nmasses exchange velocities; heavy hits light at rest → light moves at\n≈2u.</li>\n<li>Perfectly inelastic: v = (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂); KE lost =\n½[m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂)](u₁ − u₂)².</li>\n<li>Restitution e = (v₂ − v₁)/(u₁ − u₂): 1 elastic, 0 perfectly\ninelastic.</li>\n<li>Vertical circle (string): v_top,min = √(gr); v_bottom,min = √(5gr);\nT_bottom = mg + mv²/r; tension at top is zero when v = √(gr); string\ngoes slack if v_top &lt; √(gr) (25.7).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Energy questions with a pendulum: convert KE at\nthe bottom to height with h = v²/2g.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P5": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> rolling velocities (24.1), MI of rod\n(24.22), angular momentum conservation for planets (25.17), rod\nequilibrium with friction (25.22), MI of a cut sphere (25.39), MI of\nring (26.20), flywheel revolutions (26.38), angular momentum of a disc\n(R.3), parallel axis theorem (R.30).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Moments of inertia (axis through centre): ring MR²; disc ½MR²; solid\nsphere ⅖MR²; hollow sphere ⅔MR²; rod (through centre) ML²/12; rod about\nend ML²/3; solid cylinder ½MR².</li>\n<li>Parallel axis I = I_cm + Md²; perpendicular axis (planar bodies\nonly) I_z = I_x + I_y.</li>\n<li>τ = Iα; L = Iω; τ = dL/dt; rotational KE = ½Iω²; if net torque is\nzero, L is constant (ice skater, planets: Kepler's second law).</li>\n<li>Kinematics: ω = ω₀ + αt; θ = ω₀t + ½αt²; ω² = ω₀² + 2αθ (revolutions\n= θ/2π).</li>\n<li>Rolling without slipping: v_cm = ωR; top point 2v, bottom point 0\n(24.1); KE_total = ½mv²(1 + k²/R²).</li>\n<li>On an incline a = g sinθ/(1 + k²/R²); order of reaching bottom:\nsolid sphere (k²/R² = 2/5) &gt; disc (1/2) &gt; hollow sphere (2/3) &gt;\nring (1).</li>\n<li>Equilibrium: ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0 (take torques about a point where an\nunknown force acts).</li>\n<li>Centre of mass: x_cm = Σmx/Σm; COM does not move without external\nforce.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Cut sphere/disc: MI is proportional to mass, so\nfind the fraction of mass first.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P6": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> gravity on a planet (24.35), satellite\nlaunch energy (24.36), Kepler's law (25.31, R.37), gravity at height\n(25.32), work to raise a mass (26.27), escape speed of planets\n(R.10).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>F = Gm₁m₂/r²; g = GM/R²; g at height h: g′ = g(R/(R + h))², ≈ g(1 −\n2h/R) for small h; at depth d: g′ = g(1 − d/R); at the centre g =\n0.</li>\n<li>Escape speed v_e = √(2GM/R) = √(2gR) = 11.2 km/s for Earth; orbital\nspeed v_o = √(gR); v_e = √2·v_o.</li>\n<li>Satellite: KE = GMm/2r; PE = −GMm/r; total E = −GMm/2r; binding\nenergy = +GMm/2r; T² ∝ r³ (Kepler); T = 2π√(r³/GM).</li>\n<li>Potential energy at distance r: U = −GMm/r; work to lift from R to R\n+ h: W = GMmh/[R(R + h)]; for h = R this equals mgR/2 (26.27 type).</li>\n<li>Kepler: law of orbits (ellipse), areas (L conserved), periods (T² ∝\na³).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For \"which planet has larger escape speed\",\ncompute √(M/R) ratios.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P7": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> surface tension force on a disc (24.8),\nsteel wire elongation (24.13), thermal stress (24.42), surface-tension\ncurvature (25.1), heat conduction through rods in series (25.19),\nelastic modulus match (26.18), submarine pressure (26.39), Bernoulli\nstreamline flow (R.2), thermal volume expansion (R.5), terminal velocity\n(R.9).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Young's modulus Y = (F/A)/(ΔL/L); energy stored = ½ × stress ×\nstrain × volume; wire of length L, area A: extension ΔL = FL/(AY); bulk\nmodulus K = −VΔP/ΔV; shear modulus η = (F/A)/θ.</li>\n<li>Thermal stress = YαΔT; thermal expansion ΔL = LαΔT; γ = 3α; β = 2α\n(area); for liquids, volume = V₀(1 + γΔT).</li>\n<li>Pressure P = P₀ + ρgh; Pascal's law; buoyancy = ρVg; floating means\nweight = buoyancy.</li>\n<li>Continuity A₁v₁ = A₂v₂; Bernoulli P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant;\nTorricelli speed of efflux v = √(2gh).</li>\n<li>Viscosity: F = 6πηrv (Stokes); terminal velocity v_t = 2r²(ρ −\nσ)g/9η; viscosity falls with temperature for liquids and rises for\ngases.</li>\n<li>Surface tension T = F/ℓ (a disc or ring pulled from a surface has\ntwo surfaces ⇒ 2 × circumference); surface energy = T × area; excess\npressure drop 2T/R, soap bubble 4T/R; capillary rise h =\n2Tcosθ/ρgr.</li>\n<li>Heat: Q = mcΔT; Q = mL; calorimetry (heat lost = heat gained).</li>\n<li>Conduction: Q/t = kAΔT/L; series equivalent thermal resistance R =\nL/kA adds; interface temperature of two rods: k₁(T₁ − T)/L₁ = k₂(T −\nT₂)/L₂ (equal areas).</li>\n<li>Radiation: Stefan P = σAT⁴; Wien λ_m T = b; Newton's cooling rate ∝\ntemperature difference.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Wire-stretch problems: if volume is fixed and\nlength is multiplied by n, resistance (and A change) → area ÷ n.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P8": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> work in isochoric process (24.3),\nisobaric heat and internal energy (25.41), first law for a heater\n(26.28), adiabatic work for monatomic gas (R.15), cyclic process heat\n(R.43).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First law: Q = ΔU + W; ΔU = nC_vΔT (any process, ideal gas).</li>\n<li>Isothermal W = nRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 2.303 nRT log(V₂/V₁); isobaric W =\nPΔV; isochoric W = 0.</li>\n<li>Adiabatic: PVᵞ = const; TVᵞ⁻¹ = const; W = (P₁V₁ − P₂V₂)/(γ − 1) =\nnR(T₁ − T₂)/(γ − 1).</li>\n<li>C_p − C_v = R; γ = 5/3 (monatomic), 7/5 (diatomic rigid); C_v =\n3R/2, 5R/2.</li>\n<li>Isobaric heat split for an ideal gas: ΔU/Q = 1/γ, W/Q = 1 − 1/γ\n(monatomic: 60% to ΔU, 40% to work; diatomic: 71%, 29%).</li>\n<li>Cyclic process: ΔU = 0; net work = area enclosed; Q_net =\nW_net.</li>\n<li>Carnot: η = 1 − T₂/T₁ (kelvin); refrigerator COP = T₂/(T₁ −\nT₂).</li>\n<li>Second law: no 100% efficient engine; heat flows hot to cold\nspontaneously.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For a P–V loop, clockwise = work done by gas\n(positive).</li>\n</ul>\n", "P9": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> ideal gas T–V isobars (24.47), moles in\na cylinder (25.14), gas mixture equilibrium pressure (25.29), rms speed\nof argon vs chlorine (26.45), C_p/C_v with vibrational modes (R.18),\nmean free path (R.32).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PV = nRT = NkT; P = ⅓ρv²_rms.</li>\n<li>v_rms = √(3RT/M); v_avg = √(8RT/πM); v_mp = √(2RT/M); ratio v_mp :\nv_avg : v_rms = √2 : √(8/π) : √3 ≈ 1 : 1.13 : 1.22.</li>\n<li>Average KE per molecule = (3/2)kT; per mole = (3/2)RT; at the same\nT, lighter molecules move faster (rms ∝ 1/√M).</li>\n<li>Degrees of freedom f: U = (f/2)nRT; C_v = fR/2; γ = 1 + 2/f.\nMonatomic f = 3; diatomic 5 (7 if vibration counted → γ = 9/7, C_v =\n7R/2); law of equipartition: ½kT per DOF.</li>\n<li>Mean free path λ = 1/(√2·πd²n) = kT/(√2·πd²P).</li>\n<li>Dalton's law P = ΣPᵢ; mixture of gases: f_mix = (n₁f₁ + n₂f₂)/(n₁ +\nn₂); rms speed of a mixture needs the individual masses.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Same T, different gases: KE equal, but rms\nspeed depends on mass; the heavier one is slower.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P10": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> SHM amplitude/period (24.29), simple\npendulum (24.50, 26.25, 26.32, 26.36), spring–mass with leaking sand\n(25.23), spring masses amplitude ratio (25.44), open pipe dipped in\nwater (25.45), travelling wave phase difference (26.22), floating cork\nperiod (R.33), pipe harmonics (R.34), spring–mass SHM plot (R.45).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SHM: x = A sin(ωt + φ); v = ω√(A² − x²); a = −ω²x; v_max = ωA; a_max\n= ω²A.</li>\n<li>Spring: T = 2π√(m/k); series k_eq = k₁k₂/(k₁ + k₂); parallel k₁ +\nk₂; cutting a spring to 1/n length multiplies k by n.</li>\n<li>Pendulum T = 2π√(ℓ/g); ℓ from the time period ℓ = gT²/4π²; lift\naccelerating up: g_eff = g + a; in a satellite T → ∞.</li>\n<li>Energy: total E = ½kA² (constant); KE and PE oscillate at twice the\nSHM frequency.</li>\n<li>Floating cylinder/cork: T = 2π√(m/(Aρg)) = 2π√(h/g) where h is the\nsubmerged depth.</li>\n<li>Waves: v = fλ; v = √(T/μ) on a string; y = A sin(kx − ωt); phase\ndifference = 2πΔx/λ = ωΔt.</li>\n<li>Standing waves: string/open pipe fₙ = nv/2L (n = 1, 2, 3); closed\npipe fₙ = (2n − 1)v/4L (odd harmonics only); end correction e ≈\n0.6r.</li>\n<li>Resonance-tube: v = 2f(L₂ − L₁); beats f_b = |f₁ − f₂|; Doppler f′ =\nf(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s) (approach raises pitch).</li>\n<li>Intensity ∝ A²; I ∝ 1/r² for a point source.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> In a pipe problem, the number of\nnodes/antinodes follows from the harmonic: closed pipe has a node at the\nclosed end and an antinode at the open end.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P11": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> dipole axial potential (24.9),\nequivalent capacitance (24.25, 26.15, R.13), potential of a charged\nshell (24.34), parallel-plate capacitor with plates closer (24.46),\ncapacitor with dielectric slabs (25.20), charge sharing (25.28), dipole\npotential energy (25.35), energy loss on sharing (26.31), conductor\nstatements (26.41), work in a charged dielectric sphere (R.19), cavity\nin a conductor (R.41).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coulomb F = kq₁q₂/r²; E = kq/r²; V = kq/r (scalar).</li>\n<li>Dipole p = q·2a: axial E = 2kp/r³, equatorial E = kp/r³ (opposite to\np); axial V = kp/r², equatorial V = 0; torque τ = pE sinθ; U = −pE cosθ;\nwork to rotate from θ₁ to θ₂ = pE(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂).</li>\n<li>Gauss: ∮E·dA = q/ε₀. Infinite line E = λ/2πε₀r; infinite sheet E =\nσ/2ε₀; conductor surface E = σ/ε₀; spherical shell: E = 0 inside, V =\nconstant inside and equal to the surface value, outside behaves like a\npoint charge.</li>\n<li>Conductor: E = 0 inside, charge on the outer surface, surface\nequipotential; field inside a cavity (no charge in cavity) = 0.</li>\n<li>Capacitor: C = ε₀A/d; with dielectric C = Kε₀A/d; with partial slab\nC = ε₀A/(d − t + t/K).</li>\n<li>Series 1/C = Σ1/Cᵢ (same charge); parallel C = ΣCᵢ (same voltage).\nEnergy U = ½CV² = Q²/2C.</li>\n<li>Battery connected: V constant. Battery disconnected: Q constant.\nPlates brought closer: C increases; if disconnected, V falls, U falls\n(24.46).</li>\n<li>Two capacitors joined: common V = (C₁V₁ + C₂V₂)/(C₁ + C₂); energy\nloss = ½·[C₁C₂/(C₁ + C₂)](V₁ − V₂)².</li>\n<li>Force per unit area on a charged conductor σ²/2ε₀.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Capacitor network: first find which capacitors\nare in series; unequal-branch bridge networks need symmetry (balance\npoint has equal potential).</li>\n</ul>\n", "P12": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> terminal voltage (24.4, 26.26),\nseries/parallel resistance (24.32), heaters in series/parallel (24.39,\n26.29), bridge with a diode (24.44), battery circuit (25.6), resistors\nfrom a cut wire (25.33), bridge branch current (25.36), resistor network\n(26.9), meter bridge with interchanged cell and galvanometer (26.16),\ntemperature coefficient of resistance (R.6).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>V = IR; R = ρℓ/A; resistance of a stretched wire (volume fixed): R ∝\nℓ² (if length ×n, R ×n²).</li>\n<li>A wire of resistance R cut into n equal pieces and connected in\nparallel: R/n² (25.33); in series: R.</li>\n<li>R_T = R₀(1 + αΔT); semiconductors have negative α.</li>\n<li>Drift velocity v_d = I/(neA); I = neAv_d; mobility μ = v_d/E.</li>\n<li>Cell: V = E − Ir (discharging); series EMFs add; for n identical\ncells in parallel, E stays, r/n.</li>\n<li>Kirchhoff: ΣI = 0 at a node; ΣV = 0 around a loop.</li>\n<li>Wheatstone balance P/Q = R/S; meter bridge R/S = ℓ/(100 − ℓ);\ninterchanging gives R/S swapped.</li>\n<li>Power P = VI = I²R = V²/R; bulbs/heaters in series: the one with the\nhigher resistance (lower wattage) glows brighter; in parallel: higher\nwattage glows brighter.</li>\n<li>Heaters: P_series = P₁P₂/(P₁ + P₂); P_parallel = P₁ + P₂.</li>\n<li>Max power transfer when the external resistance equals the internal\nresistance.</li>\n<li>Ideal diode in a circuit: forward-biased = a wire; reverse-biased =\nan open switch (24.44).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Symmetrical networks: points at equal potential\ncan be joined or removed; no current flows between them.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P13": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> magnetic needle oscillation (24.10),\nfield at coil centre (24.21, 26.1, R.20), magnetic susceptibility\n(24.24), bent magnet moment (24.49), velocity selector (25.3), magnetic\nmoment of loops (25.12), electron magnetic moment (25.18), B inside a\ncurrent-carrying wire (26.8), shunt for an ammeter (26.12), force\nbetween parallel wires (R.44).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biot–Savart: loop centre B = μ₀NI/2R; on axis B = μ₀NIR²/[2(R² +\nx²)^{3/2}]; arc of angle θ: B = μ₀Iθ/4πR; long straight wire B =\nμ₀I/2πr; solenoid B = μ₀nI; inside a straight wire of radius a (uniform\ncurrent): B = μ₀Ir/2πa² (zero at the axis, maximum at the surface).</li>\n<li>Force on a charge F = q(E + v × B); circular path r = mv/qB =\n√(2mK)/qB; T = 2πm/qB (independent of speed); pitch of a helix =\nv∥T.</li>\n<li>Velocity selector (E ⟂ B): v = E/B passes undeflected.</li>\n<li>Force on a wire F = BIℓ sinθ; parallel wires F/ℓ = μ₀I₁I₂/2πd (same\ndirection attract).</li>\n<li>Loop: moment M = NIA; torque τ = MB sinθ; U = −MB cosθ; magnet\nbar-field axis B = μ₀·2M/4πr³.</li>\n<li>Moving-coil galvanometer: shunt S = I_gG/(I − I_g) for an ammeter;\nseries resistance R = V/I_g − G for a voltmeter; current sensitivity θ/I\n= NAB/C.</li>\n<li>Oscillating magnet: T = 2π√(I/MB).</li>\n<li>Materials: diamagnetic χ small negative; paramagnetic χ small\npositive ∝ 1/T (Curie law); ferromagnetic χ large positive, above Curie\ntemperature becomes paramagnetic; μ_r = 1 + χ.</li>\n<li>Orbital magnetic moment of an electron μ = eL/2m; Bohr magneton =\neħ/2m.</li>\n<li>A bar magnet bent at its midpoint into an L shape: moment becomes\nM/√2; cut across the length: M halves; cut along the length: M\nhalves.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Right-hand rule for direction; for a coil, N\nturns multiplies the field at the centre by N.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P14": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> ideal transformer (24.5), Lenz's law\nwith a magnet (24.30), capacitor in AC (24.41), eddy currents (24.45),\ninductor potential difference (25.11), series LCR (25.16, R.11), LCR\nresonance (26.7), motional emf (26.11), AC peak value timing (26.33),\nsolenoid self-inductance (R.4), series/parallel inductors (R.12),\nFaraday induced power (R.14).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Faraday ε = −dΦ/dt; motional ε = Bℓv; rotating rod ε = ½Bωℓ²;\ninduced charge q = ΔΦ/R; power in a loop P = (Bℓv)²/R.</li>\n<li>Self inductance L = μ₀N²A/ℓ; energy ½LI²; mutual inductance M;\ninductors in series L₁ + L₂ (no coupling), parallel 1/L = Σ1/Lᵢ.</li>\n<li>LR growth: I = I₀(1 − e^{−t/τ}), τ = L/R.</li>\n<li>Lenz: induced current opposes the change; eddy currents damp motion\n(induction furnace, magnetic braking).</li>\n<li>AC: I_rms = I₀/√2; average over a half cycle = 2I₀/π; X_L = ωL; X_C\n= 1/ωC; Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²); tanφ = (X_L − X_C)/R; power P =\nV_rms·I_rms·cosφ; cosφ = R/Z; wattless current when φ = 90°.</li>\n<li>Resonance: ω₀ = 1/√(LC); at resonance Z = R (minimum), I maximum, φ\n= 0; quality factor Q = ω₀L/R.</li>\n<li>Capacitor in AC: I₀ = V₀ωC, current leads voltage by 90°; inductor:\ncurrent lags by 90°.</li>\n<li>Transformer (ideal): V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p = I_p/I_s; step-up has more\nturns on the secondary.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For \"peak value reached at time t\", solve\nsin(ωt) = 1 → t = T/4.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P15": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> displacement current (24.43, 25.26), EM\nwave properties (24.48), EM wave field expression (25.9), production\nmatch (26.40), spectrum applications (R.17), wave in a dielectric\n(R.22).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀) = 3 × 10⁸ m/s; E₀/B₀ = c; in a medium v = c/√(μ_rε_r)\n(frequency stays, λ shrinks).</li>\n<li>Displacement current I_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt (flows between capacitor\nplates; equal to the conduction current).</li>\n<li>Spectrum in increasing frequency: radio &lt; microwave &lt; infrared\n&lt; visible &lt; ultraviolet &lt; X-ray &lt; gamma. Uses: microwave\n(radar, oven), infrared (remote, greenhouse effect), UV (sterilisation,\nLASIK), X-ray (imaging), gamma (cancer therapy).</li>\n<li>Electric and magnetic energy densities are equal: u = ½ε₀E² =\nB²/2μ₀; intensity I = ½ε₀cE₀²; momentum p = U/c.</li>\n<li>EM waves are transverse and need no medium; produced by accelerating\ncharges.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Wave equation check: E and B oscillate in phase\nand perpendicular; direction of travel = E × B.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P16": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> prism refractive index (24.6, 26.23),\nYDSE with white light (24.11), Brewster angle (24.19, 25.27), telescope\nmagnifying power (24.37), compound microscope (25.2), thin lens\ncombination (25.13, R.29), Malus law (25.43), YDSE intensity (26.13),\nconcave lens ray (26.19), interference/diffraction statements (26.34),\napparent depth (R.24).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mirror 1/v + 1/u = 1/f; lens 1/v − 1/u = 1/f (sign convention:\ndistances measured from the optic centre, direction of incident light\npositive).</li>\n<li>Lens maker 1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂); power P = 1/f (metres);\nlenses in contact P = P₁ + P₂; separated by d: 1/f = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂ −\nd/f₁f₂.</li>\n<li>Magnification m = v/u (lens), −v/u (mirror).</li>\n<li>Refraction n = sin i/sin r; apparent depth = real depth/n; critical\nangle sinC = 1/n.</li>\n<li>Prism: n = sin[(A + δ_m)/2]/sin(A/2); small angle δ = (n − 1)A.</li>\n<li>Microscope: M ≈ (L/f_o)(D/f_e) at final image at infinity; telescope\nM = f_o/f_e, tube length f_o + f_e.</li>\n<li>Young's: fringe width β = λD/d; path difference = d sinθ = yd/D;\nmaxima at nλ, minima at (n + ½)λ; Iₘₐₓ/Iₘᵢₙ = (√I₁ + √I₂)²/(√I₁ − √I₂)²;\nwith white light the central fringe is white and the first colour fringe\nhas violet nearest the centre.</li>\n<li>Single slit: minima at a sinθ = nλ; central maximum width 2λD/a\n(twice the others).</li>\n<li>Brewster tan i_p = n; reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular\nat i_p; reflected light fully polarised.</li>\n<li>Malus I = I₀cos²θ; unpolarised light through one polariser gives\nI₀/2.</li>\n<li>Interference and diffraction are redistribution of energy, not loss\n(26.34).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For lens combinations, find the first image and\ntreat it as the object for the second lens.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P17": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> photon properties (24.16), de Broglie\nwavelength–energy graph (24.18), de Broglie for n = 2 (25.34), photon vs\nelectron de Broglie ratio (25.37, R.1), photoelectric current variation\n(25.38), match-list (26.2), work function and wavelength (26.42),\nphotocurrent vs stopping potential (R.7), threshold and stopping\npotential (R.39).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Photon E = hν = hc/λ; E (eV) = 1240/λ (nm); momentum p = h/λ =\nE/c.</li>\n<li>Photoelectric: KE_max = hν − φ; eV₀ = KE_max; threshold ν₀ = φ/h.\nEmission is instantaneous, depends on frequency not intensity.</li>\n<li>Photocurrent ∝ intensity (above threshold); stopping potential\ndepends only on frequency and the metal; V₀–ν graph slope = h/e;\nintercept on the V₀ axis = −φ/e.</li>\n<li>de Broglie λ = h/p = h/√(2mK) = h/√(2mqV); electron λ = 12.27/√V Å;\nsame K: λ ∝ 1/√m (so proton has a shorter λ than an electron); same V: λ\n∝ 1/√(mq).</li>\n<li>Electron and photon of equal λ have equal momentum; photon KE = pc,\nelectron KE = p²/2m.</li>\n<li>Davisson–Germer confirmed matter waves.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For a \"match list\" question, link each pair: E\n= hν ↔︎ particle nature, λ = h/p ↔︎ de Broglie, diffraction ↔︎ wave nature,\nCompton effect ↔︎ photon momentum.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P18": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> hydrogen spectral lines (24.2), emission\nspectrum (24.12), alpha/beta decay chain (24.27), Bohr model (25.30,\n26.43, R.31), nuclear density (26.10), nucleus volume and mass defect\n(26.37), alpha decay Q value (R.35), Geiger–Marsden plot (R.42).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bohr: r_n = 0.529n²/Z Å; v_n = 2.19 × 10⁶ Z/n m/s; E_n = −13.6Z²/n²\neV; ΔE = hc/λ.</li>\n<li>Rydberg 1/λ = RZ²(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²); R = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹.</li>\n<li>Series: Lyman → n = 1 (UV); Balmer → n = 2 (visible); Paschen → n =\n3 (IR). Balmer lines: 3→2 656.3 nm, 4→2 486.1 nm, 5→2 434.1 nm, 6→2\n410.2 nm (24.2).</li>\n<li>Number of spectral lines from level n = n(n − 1)/2.</li>\n<li>Nucleus: R = R₀A^{1/3}, R₀ ≈ 1.2 fm; nuclear density is constant (≈\n2.3 × 10¹⁷ kg/m³), independent of A; volume ∝ A.</li>\n<li>Mass defect Δm = Zm_p + (A − Z)m_n − M; B.E. = Δm × 931.5 MeV; B.E.\nper nucleon peaks near Fe-56 (~8.7–8.8 MeV): light nuclei fuse, heavy\nnuclei fission.</li>\n<li>Decay: N = N₀e^{−λt}; T½ = 0.693/λ; mean life τ = 1/λ; activity A =\nλN; after n half-lives N = N₀/2ⁿ.</li>\n<li>α emission: A − 4, Z − 2; β⁻: Z + 1; β⁺: Z − 1; γ: no change.\nQ-value = (M_parent − M_daughter − M_α)c².</li>\n<li>Rutherford: distance of closest approach r₀ = 2Ze²/(4πε₀K); most α\nparticles go undeviated; very few (1 in 8000) deflected through large\nangles.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For decay chains, count α (4 mass units each)\nfrom ΔA, then fix Z with β.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P19": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> solar cell in reverse (24.14), logic\ngate expressions (24.31, 24.33, 25.8), full-wave rectifier (25.40),\nideal diodes circuit (26.3), diode output waveform (26.24), bias\nstatements (26.44), Zener (R.8), depletion width (R.28).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Semiconductor gaps: Ge 0.67 eV, Si 1.1 eV; conductors overlap,\ninsulators &gt; 3 eV.</li>\n<li>n-type: pentavalent donor (P, As, Sb); p-type: trivalent acceptor\n(B, Al, Ga, In); nᵢ² = np; the whole crystal stays neutral.</li>\n<li>Diode: forward bias reduces depletion width, conducts above ~0.7 V\n(Si), ~0.3 V (Ge); reverse bias widens the depletion layer.</li>\n<li>Half-wave rectifier output frequency = input frequency; full-wave\noutput frequency = 2 × input.</li>\n<li>Zener: used in reverse breakdown as a voltage regulator. LED:\nforward-biased, emits light. Photodiode: reverse-biased, current ∝\nlight. Solar cell: no external bias; works in the fourth quadrant.</li>\n<li>Logic gates: OR A + B; AND A·B; NOT A′; NAND (A·B)′; NOR (A + B)′.\nNAND and NOR are universal gates.</li>\n<li>De Morgan: (A + B)′ = A′B′; (AB)′ = A′ + B′.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For a gate circuit, write the Boolean output\nstep by step and then fill the truth table.</li>\n</ul>\n", "P20": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> vernier constant (24.28), vernier zero\nerror (25.25, R.40), least count (26.6), index correction (R.36).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vernier LC = 1 MSD − 1 VSD = (value of 1 MSD)/N; reading = MSR + VSR\n× LC − zero error.</li>\n<li>Screw gauge LC = pitch/number of circular-scale divisions; reading =\nPSR + CSR × LC − zero error. Zero error is positive if the zero of the\ncircular scale is below the reference line (subtract it); negative zero\nerror is added.</li>\n<li>Simple pendulum g = 4π²L/T²; graph of T² vs L is a straight line\nwith slope 4π²/g.</li>\n<li>Metre bridge R = S·ℓ/(100 − ℓ); resistivity ρ = RA/ℓ.</li>\n<li>Half-deflection method: G = RS/(R − S); figure of merit k = E/[(R +\nG)θ].</li>\n<li>Resonance tube v = 2f(ℓ₂ − ℓ₁); Young's modulus from the slope of\nload–extension; surface tension by capillary rise; viscosity from\nterminal velocity; specific heat by the method of mixtures; focal length\nby the parallax method (u = v = 2f gives f).</li>\n<li>Diode characteristics: forward and reverse curves; Zener reverse\nbreakdown voltage.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> If the question gives a zero error, correct the\nreading first, then apply other steps.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C1": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> mole of helium atoms (24.69), limiting\nreagent (24.78), empirical formula (24.90), Dalton limitations (25.54),\natoms in a mole (25.68), CO₂ over coke (26.49), H atoms in urea (26.65),\nsignificant figures (R.62), butane combustion (R.63).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>n = m/M = N/N_A = V/22.4 L (at STP, 1 atm, 273 K) (22.7 L at 1 bar);\nN_A = 6.022 × 10²³.</li>\n<li>Molarity M = n/V(L) (changes with temperature); molality m = n/kg\nsolvent (no change with temperature); mole fraction x = nᵢ/Σn; % by\nmass.</li>\n<li>Limiting reagent: divide moles by the stoichiometric coefficient;\nthe smallest ratio is limiting.</li>\n<li>Empirical formula: % → moles → divide by smallest → whole ratio;\nmolecular formula = n × empirical (n = molar mass/empirical mass).</li>\n<li>Count atoms: urea CO(NH₂)₂ = CH₄N₂O has 4 H per molecule.</li>\n<li>Laws: conservation of mass, definite proportions, multiple\nproportions, Gay-Lussac's gaseous volumes, Avogadro.</li>\n<li>Dalton's limits: cannot explain isotopes, allotropes, sub-atomic\nparticles; the atom is divisible.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Always convert everything to moles first;\nratios of moles give answers.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C2": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> Bohr energy (24.52, 25.50), quantum\nnumbers (24.83, 26.50), hydrogen spectrum ratio (25.46), photons from a\nbulb (26.59), radial nodes (R.64).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bohr: Eₙ = −13.6Z²/n² eV (−2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ Z²/n² J); rₙ = 52.9n²/Z pm;\nvₙ ∝ Z/n.</li>\n<li>Rydberg 1/λ = R_H Z²(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²); Lyman (→1) UV, Balmer (→2)\nvisible, Paschen (→3) IR; for hydrogen-like species λ ∝ 1/Z².</li>\n<li>Quantum numbers: n ≥ 1; l = 0 … n − 1; m = −l … +l; s = ±½. Orbitals\nin a shell n²; electrons 2n²; orbitals in a subshell 2l + 1.</li>\n<li>Nodes: radial nodes = n − l − 1; angular nodes = l; total = n −\n1.</li>\n<li>Aufbau (increasing n + l, ties lower n first); Pauli; Hund.\nExceptions: Cr [Ar]3d⁵4s¹; Cu [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹.</li>\n<li>de Broglie λ = h/mv; Heisenberg Δx·Δp ≥ h/4π; photons emitted n =\nPλt/hc.</li>\n<li>Photoelectric: KE = hν − hν₀.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Nodes: write \"n − l − 1\" for radial, \"l\" for\nangular; plug in.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C3": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> σ/π bonds (24.67, 26.69), intramolecular\nH-bonding (24.68), hydride boiling points (24.79), shapes (24.84, 26.54,\nR.88), dipole moment (24.87, 25.87), bond order (25.62), formal charge\nof ozone (26.86), MO of Ne₂ (R.87).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hybridisation H = ½(V + M − C + A) (V valence e⁻ of central atom, M\nmonovalent atoms, C cation charge, A anion charge).</li>\n<li>VSEPR: 2 pairs linear; 3 trigonal planar; 4 tetrahedral (CH₄), NH₃\npyramidal (107°), H₂O bent (104.5°); 5 trigonal bipyramidal (PCl₅), SF₄\nsee-saw, ClF₃ T-shaped, XeF₂ linear; 6 octahedral (SF₆), BrF₅ square\npyramidal, XeF₄ square planar; 7 pentagonal bipyramidal (IF₇).</li>\n<li>Bond order = (N_b − N_a)/2. MO order: for O₂, F₂, Ne₂: σ2p below\nπ2p; for B₂, C₂, N₂ (and lighter): π2p below σ2p.</li>\n<li>Bond orders and magnetism: N₂ 3 (dia); N₂⁺ 2.5 (para); O₂⁺ 2.5\n(para); O₂ 2 (para); O₂⁻ 1.5 (para); O₂²⁻ 1 (dia); C₂ 2 (dia); B₂ 1\n(para); Ne₂ 0 (does not exist). Stability follows bond order: O₂⁺ &gt;\nO₂ &gt; O₂⁻ &gt; O₂²⁻.</li>\n<li>σ/π count: single = 1σ; double = 1σ + 1π; triple = 1σ + 2π.</li>\n<li>Dipole moment μ = q × d; zero for CO₂, BF₃, CH₄, CCl₄, XeF₄, SF₆;\nNH₃ &gt; NF₃ (lone pair and bond moments oppose in NF₃); cis &gt; trans;\northo &gt; meta &gt; para in disubstituted benzenes with identical\ngroups.</li>\n<li>Fajans: small, highly charged cation + large anion → more covalent.\nFormal charge = V − N − B/2.</li>\n<li>Hydrogen bonding: intramolecular (o-nitrophenol, chelation; more\nvolatile, lower b.p.) vs intermolecular (p-isomer; higher b.p.). Hydride\nb.p.: H₂O &gt; HF &gt; NH₃ (anomalous); then rises down the group for\nothers.</li>\n<li>Bond length: triple &lt; double &lt; single; bond energy\nreverse.</li>\n<li>Bond angle: CH₄ 109.5°, NH₃ 107°, H₂O 104.5°, BeCl₂ 180°, BF₃\n120°.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For MOT questions, count total electrons first;\nadd one electron to π* (O₂⁻), remove from π* (O₂⁺).</li>\n</ul>\n", "C4": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> process conditions (24.54), entropy\nincrease (24.57, R.59), isothermal reversible work (24.86, R.80),\nformation enthalpy diagram (25.65, 25.85), first law internal energy\n(26.70), ΔG from ΔU (26.85), denaturation entropy (R.83).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ΔU = q + w (IUPAC); w = −P_extΔV; isothermal reversible w =\n−2.303nRT log(V₂/V₁); |w_rev| &gt; |w_irrev| for expansion.</li>\n<li>ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT; C_p − C_v = R; ΔH reaction by bond enthalpies =\nΣ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed); Hess's law.</li>\n<li>ΔG = ΔH − TΔS; ΔG° = −2.303RT log K = −nFE°; ΔS_surr = −ΔH/T; at\nequilibrium T = ΔH/ΔS.</li>\n<li>Spontaneity: ΔH − and ΔS + → always; ΔH + and ΔS − → never; ΔH − and\nΔS − → low T; ΔH + and ΔS + → high T.</li>\n<li>Free expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum: w = 0, q = 0, ΔU = 0,\nbut ΔS &gt; 0 (R.59).</li>\n<li>State functions: U, H, S, G, T, P, V; not q or w. Extensive: U, H,\nS, G, V; intensive: T, P, density.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> If Δn_g = 0, ΔH = ΔU.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C5": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> osmotic pressure (24.99), types of\nsolutions (25.66), boiling-point elevation with van't Hoff factor\n(25.79), non-ideal deviation (25.81), Henry statements (26.76), negative\ndeviation chloroform–acetone (26.77), ideal solution (R.69, R.81).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Raoult p = x p°; relative lowering of vapour pressure = mole\nfraction of solute.</li>\n<li>ΔT_b = iK_bm; ΔT_f = iK_fm; π = iCRT; molar mass M = K_b × w ×\n1000/(ΔT_b × W).</li>\n<li>van't Hoff factor: dissociation i = 1 + α(n − 1); association i = 1\n− α(1 − 1/n); i &gt; 1 dissociation, i &lt; 1 association.</li>\n<li>Isotonic solutions have equal π; reverse osmosis needs pressure &gt;\nπ.</li>\n<li>Henry: p = K_H x (higher K_H, lower solubility); solubility of gases\nfalls with temperature.</li>\n<li>Positive deviation (ethanol–water, ethanol–acetone,\nbenzene–ethanol): ΔH_mix &gt; 0, ΔV_mix &gt; 0, forms minimum-boiling\nazeotrope. Negative deviation (chloroform–acetone, HNO₃–water,\nHCl–water): ΔH_mix &lt; 0, ΔV_mix &lt; 0, maximum-boiling azeotrope\n(26.77). Ideal: ΔH_mix = ΔV_mix = 0 (R.81).</li>\n<li>Molality does not change with temperature; molarity does. ppm = mass\nof solute × 10⁶/mass of solution.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For boiling-point elevation, count particles\nfirst (i), then compare.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C6": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> reaction quotient (24.55), K_p–K_c\n(24.60, 26.87), Henry's law (24.74), degree of dissociation (24.92),\npolyprotic acid (25.59), K_p from rate constants (25.73), Le Chatelier\n(25.88), pH from K_sp (26.48), buffer pH (26.78), K_sp comparison\n(R.78).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>K_p = K_c(RT)^Δn; Q &lt; K → forward; Q &gt; K → reverse; ΔG° = −RT\nln K.</li>\n<li>Le Chatelier: heating favours the endothermic direction; adding an\ninert gas at constant volume has no effect, at constant pressure shifts\ntoward more moles; catalyst does not shift equilibrium.</li>\n<li>pH = −log[H⁺]; pH + pOH = 14 at 25 °C; K_w = 10⁻¹⁴; K_a × K_b =\nK_w.</li>\n<li>Weak acid [H⁺] = √(K_aC); α = √(K_a/C); weak base [OH⁻] =\n√(K_bC).</li>\n<li>Buffers: pH = pKₐ + log([salt]/[acid]); pOH = pK_b +\nlog([salt]/[base]); maximum buffer capacity at pH = pKₐ.</li>\n<li>Salt pH: WA + SB: pH = 7 + ½(pKₐ + log C); SA + WB: pH = 7 − ½(pK_b\n+ log C); WA + WB: pH = 7 + ½(pKₐ − pK_b).</li>\n<li>Solubility from K_sp: AB s = √K_sp; AB₂ s = (K_sp/4)^{1/3}; A₂B₃ s =\n(K_sp/108)^{1/5}; common ion lowers solubility; precipitation when Q\n&gt; K_sp.</li>\n<li>Polyprotic: K_a1 ≫ K_a2; [H⁺] comes almost entirely from the first\nstep.</li>\n<li>For K = k_f/k_r (elementary reversible step) (25.73).</li>\n<li>Physical equilibria: Henry's law, vapour pressure; Lewis acid\n(electron pair acceptor), Brønsted (proton donor).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Degree of dissociation: set up ICE table; for\nsmall α, 1 − α ≈ 1.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C7": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> non-redox reaction (24.53), Faraday\n(24.70, 24.96, 26.73), oxidation state (25.55, R.79), molar conductivity\n(25.61), Nernst/half-cell emf (26.55), standard electrode potential\n(R.67), Kohlrausch (R.70).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxidation states: O −2 (peroxide −1, superoxide −½, OF₂ +2); H +1\n(−1 in metal hydrides); sum = charge.</li>\n<li>Balancing: ion–electron method (acidic: add H⁺/H₂O; basic: add\nOH⁻).</li>\n<li>E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode; ΔG° = −nFE°; log K = nE°/0.0592 (298\nK); Nernst E = E° − (0.0592/n) log Q.</li>\n<li>Faraday: W = ZIt = (E/96500)It; 1 F = 96500 C = 1 mol e⁻. Cu²⁺ needs\n2 F per mol; Al³⁺ 3 F per mol; Ag⁺ 1 F.</li>\n<li>Conductance: κ = G(ℓ/A); Λₘ = κ × 1000/C; strong electrolytes Λₘ =\nΛ°ₘ − A√C; weak electrolytes α = Λₘ/Λ°ₘ; Kohlrausch: Λ°ₘ = ν₊λ°₊ +\nν₋λ°₋.</li>\n<li>Electrolysis products: aqueous CuSO₄ (inert electrodes) → Cu at\ncathode, O₂ at anode; dilute H₂SO₄ → H₂ and O₂; aqueous NaCl → H₂ at\ncathode, Cl₂ at anode.</li>\n<li>Strongest reducing agent Li (E° −3.05 V); strongest oxidising F₂\n(+2.87 V); SHE = 0.</li>\n<li>Batteries: dry cell (Zn, MnO₂), lead accumulator (discharge: Pb,\nPbO₂, H₂SO₄ → PbSO₄), fuel cell H₂–O₂ (efficiency ~70%).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> A \"non-redox\" reaction (24.53) is one where\nevery oxidation number is unchanged; check each element.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C8": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> activation energy (24.81, 24.93, 26.88),\nArrhenius plot (24.85, R.49), first-order time (25.56, 25.89), units of\nk (26.56), order from plot (26.83), zero-order time (R.77).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rate = k[A]^m[B]^n; unit of k = (mol L⁻¹)^{1−n} s⁻¹ (zero: mol\nL⁻¹s⁻¹; first: s⁻¹; second: L mol⁻¹s⁻¹).</li>\n<li>Zero order: [A] = [A]₀ − kt; t½ = [A]₀/2k; completes in [A]₀/k.</li>\n<li>First order: k = (2.303/t) log([A]₀/[A]); t½ = 0.693/k (independent\nof concentration); t₇₅ = 2t½; t₉₉.₉ = 10t½; N = N₀/2ⁿ with n =\nt/t½.</li>\n<li>Arrhenius k = Ae^{−E_a/RT}; log k vs 1/T slope = −E_a/2.303R; ln k\nvs 1/T slope = −E_a/R; log(k₂/k₁) = (E_a/2.303R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂).</li>\n<li>Plots: zero order [A] vs t linear; first order ln[A] vs t\nlinear.</li>\n<li>Molecularity of elementary steps is 1, 2, 3 (whole numbers); order\ncan be 0, fractional.</li>\n<li>A catalyst lowers E_a for forward and reverse equally and does not\nalter K.</li>\n<li>Rule of thumb: rate roughly doubles per 10 °C.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> If t½ is fixed, you have a first-order\nreaction.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C9": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> ionisation enthalpy (24.80, R.85),\nelectronegativity (24.82), periodic property statements (25.47, 26.79),\nconfigurations (25.53), metallic character (26.80), electron gain\nenthalpy (R.54).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atomic radius decreases across a period, increases down a group;\nionisation enthalpy increases across with exceptions (Be &gt; B, N &gt;\nO, Mg &gt; Al, P &gt; S) and decreases down.</li>\n<li>Period 2 IE order: Li &lt; B &lt; Be &lt; C &lt; O &lt; N &lt; F\n&lt; Ne.</li>\n<li>Electron gain enthalpy (most negative): Cl &gt; F &gt; Br &gt; I;\nnoble gases positive; S more negative than O.</li>\n<li>Electronegativity: F 4.0 &gt; O 3.5 &gt; N = Cl 3.0 &gt; Br\n2.8.</li>\n<li>Isoelectronic ions: higher nuclear charge → smaller (N³⁻ &gt; O²⁻\n&gt; F⁻ &gt; Na⁺ &gt; Mg²⁺ &gt; Al³⁺).</li>\n<li>Oxides: basic (left) → amphoteric (Al₂O₃, ZnO, BeO) → acidic\n(right).</li>\n<li>Diagonal pairs: Li–Mg, Be–Al, B–Si.</li>\n<li>Nomenclature digits (IUPAC for Z &gt; 100): 0 nil, 1 un, 2 bi, 3\ntri, 4 quad, 5 pent, 6 hex, 7 sept, 8 oct, 9 enn.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Write the period-2 IE order from memory and\ncompare any question with it.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C10": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> group 16 oxidation states (24.71), group\n15 hydrides/oxides (25.52, 26.67), xenon compounds (25.64), p-block\nstatements (26.62), halides with conc. H₂SO₄/MnO₂ (R.53), potash alum\n(R.68), boron covalency (R.75).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Group 13:</strong> B is a non-metal, electron deficient (BF₃\nis a Lewis acid); boric acid B(OH)₃ + H₂O → [B(OH)₄]⁻ + H⁺ (Lewis acid,\nmonobasic); diborane B₂H₆ has two 3c–2e bridge bonds; maximum covalency\nof B is 4 (no d orbitals); inert-pair effect: Tl⁺ more stable than Tl³⁺;\nAl is amphoteric.</li>\n<li><strong>Group 14:</strong> catenation C ≫ Si; allotropes diamond\n(sp³), graphite (sp², conductor), fullerene C₆₀ (sp²).</li>\n<li><strong>Group 15:</strong> N₂ inert; hydride basic strength NH₃ &gt;\nPH₃ &gt; AsH₃ &gt; SbH₃ &gt; BiH₃; bond angle falls down the group; NH₃\nhas the anomalously high boiling point; oxoacids H₃PO₂ (monobasic,\nreducing), H₃PO₃ (dibasic), H₃PO₄ (tribasic).</li>\n<li><strong>Group 16:</strong> ozone O₃ oxidises PbS → PbSO₄; SO₂ is a\nreducing, bleaching agent; H₂SO₄ by the contact process (V₂O₅ catalyst);\noxidation states +2, +4, +6; acidic strength H₂O &lt; H₂S &lt; H₂Se &lt;\nH₂Te.</li>\n<li><strong>Group 17:</strong> F₂ is the strongest oxidiser; acid\nstrength HF &lt; HCl &lt; HBr &lt; HI; oxoacid strength HClO &lt; HClO₂\n&lt; HClO₃ &lt; HClO₄; Cl₂ + cold dilute NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl; Cl₂ + hot\nconc. NaOH → NaCl + NaClO₃; NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ → HCl, while NaBr/NaI\ngive Br₂/I₂; interhalogens ClF₃ (T-shaped), IF₇ (pentagonal\nbipyramidal).</li>\n<li><strong>Group 18:</strong> XeF₂ linear (sp³d), XeF₄ square planar\n(sp³d²), XeF₆ distorted octahedral (sp³d³), XeO₃ pyramidal (sp³), XeOF₄\nsquare pyramidal; He has the lowest boiling point; first noble gas\ncompound XePtF₆.</li>\n<li>Potash alum K₂SO₄·Al₂(SO₄)₃·24H₂O: K⁺ : Al³⁺ : SO₄²⁻ = 1 : 1 : 2\n(R.68).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Hybridisation of Xe fluorides = (8 + number of\nF)/2 electron pairs → sp³d, sp³d², sp³d³.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C11": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> E° Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺ (24.64), spin-only moment\n(24.72, 26.57, R.82), lanthanoid diamagnetism (24.89), paramagnetism\n(25.71), catalysts (26.53), Ce⁴⁺ (26.63), lanthanide unpaired electrons\n(R.48), KMnO₄ heating (R.50), melting points of 3d metals (R.84).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spin-only μ = √(n(n + 2)) BM: n = 1 → 1.73; 2 → 2.83; 3 → 3.87; 4 →\n4.90; 5 → 5.92. Ti²⁺ (d², n = 2) → 2.83 BM.</li>\n<li>3d configurations: Cr 3d⁵4s¹; Cu 3d¹⁰4s¹. Zn, Cd, Hg (d¹⁰) are not\ntransition elements. Maximum oxidation state in the 3d series: Mn\n+7.</li>\n<li>E° Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺ = +1.57 V (highest in 3d, since Mn²⁺ is the stable\nd⁵).</li>\n<li>Melting points in 3d: rise to V–Cr, dip at Mn (half-filled\nstability, weak metallic bonding), lowest for Zn (R.84).</li>\n<li>Catalysts: Fe (Haber), V₂O₅ (contact), Ni (hydrogenation), TiCl₄ +\nAl(C₂H₅)₃ (Ziegler–Natta), PdCl₂ (Wacker), MnO₂ (KClO₃\ndecomposition).</li>\n<li>KMnO₄: 2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂ (heating at 513 K) (R.50). As\noxidant: acidic MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O (n = 5); neutral/weakly\nalkaline → MnO₂ (n = 3); strongly alkaline → MnO₄²⁻ (n = 1).</li>\n<li>K₂Cr₂O₇: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O; chromate (yellow,\nCrO₄²⁻) ⇌ dichromate (orange, Cr₂O₇²⁻) by pH.</li>\n<li>Lanthanoid contraction: Zr and Hf have similar radii; basicity of\nLn(OH)₃ falls from La to Lu.</li>\n<li>Ln³⁺ f-electron counts: La f⁰, Nd f³, Gd f⁷, Lu f¹⁴; diamagnetic:\nLa³⁺, Lu³⁺, Ce⁴⁺ (f⁰); Ce⁴⁺ is a strong oxidant; Eu²⁺ (f⁷), Yb²⁺ (f¹⁴).\nActinoids: radioactive, oxidation states up to +7.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For paramagnetism questions, write the d- or\nf-configuration, count unpaired electrons, then use √(n(n + 2)).</li>\n</ul>\n", "C12": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> isomerism (24.56, R.55, R.61),\noctahedral magnetism (24.77), homoleptic/heteroleptic (24.98),\nparamagnetic Ni complexes (25.51), crystal field absorption (25.57),\nionic conductance (25.63, R.66), geometry (26.82), ambidentate (26.84),\ncoordination isomerism (26.90), nomenclature (R.47), spectrochemical\nseries (R.72), spin-only moment (R.82).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ligands: monodentate NH₃, H₂O, CN⁻, Cl⁻; bidentate en, oxalate;\nhexadentate EDTA; ambidentate NO₂⁻ (N or O), SCN⁻ (S or N), CN⁻ (C or\nN).</li>\n<li>Naming: ligands alphabetically, anionic ligands end in -o (chlorido,\ncyanido), cation complex then metal oxidation state in Roman numerals;\nanionic complex: metal name ends in -ate (ferrate, cuprate, argentate,\naurate, stannate).</li>\n<li>Isomers: geometrical (square planar MA₂B₂; octahedral MA₄B₂\ncis/trans, MA₃B₃ fac/mer); optical (octahedral [M(AA)₃],\ncis-[M(AA)₂B₂]); ionisation, hydrate, linkage, coordination (e.g.\n[Co(NH₃)₆][Cr(CN)₆]). Tetrahedral complexes show no geometrical\nisomerism.</li>\n<li>Hybridisation/magnetism: strong-field ligands (CN⁻, CO, en) →\nlow-spin, inner-orbital d²sp³ (octahedral); weak-field (H₂O, F⁻,\nhalides) → high-spin, outer-orbital sp³d². [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ diamagnetic,\n[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ 1 unpaired e⁻; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ dsp² square planar, diamagnetic;\n[NiCl₄]²⁻ sp³ tetrahedral, paramagnetic; Ni(CO)₄ sp³, diamagnetic.</li>\n<li>CFT: Δ_t = (4/9)Δ_o; Δ_o &gt; P → low spin; CFSE (octahedral) =\n−0.4n(t₂g) + 0.6n(e_g) in Δ_o.</li>\n<li>Spectrochemical series: I⁻ &lt; Br⁻ &lt; S²⁻ &lt; SCN⁻ &lt; Cl⁻ &lt;\nNO₃⁻ &lt; F⁻ &lt; OH⁻ &lt; C₂O₄²⁻ &lt; H₂O &lt; NCS⁻ &lt; EDTA⁴⁻ &lt;\nNH₃ &lt; en &lt; bpy &lt; phen &lt; NO₂⁻ &lt; CN⁻ &lt; CO.</li>\n<li>Colour: a complex absorbs the complementary colour; larger Δ →\nabsorbs shorter wavelength (λ ∝ 1/Δ).</li>\n<li>Conductivity (ions): [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ 4; [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ 3;\n[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl 2; [Co(NH₃)₃Cl₃] 0. Free (ionisable) Cl⁻ = moles of AgCl\nprecipitated.</li>\n<li>Metal carbonyls: synergic bonding (σ donation + π back-donation)\nstrengthens M–C and weakens C≡O.</li>\n<li>Importance: chelate effect, qualitative analysis, metal extraction,\nhaemoglobin (Fe), chlorophyll (Mg), vitamin B₁₂ (Co).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Oxidation state first, then d-count, then\nligand strength, then spin and magnetism.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C13": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> sublimation (24.65), separation methods\n(25.72, 26.58), Lassaigne (25.90, 26.64, R.73).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methods: crystallisation, sublimation (naphthalene, camphor), simple\ndistillation (large b.p. gap), fractional distillation, vacuum\ndistillation (glycerol), steam distillation (aniline, nitrobenzene),\ndifferential extraction, chromatography (R_f = solute distance/solvent\ndistance).</li>\n<li>Lassaigne: Na fusion makes NaCN, Na₂S, NaX. N: FeSO₄ → Prussian\nblue; S: sodium nitroprusside → violet (or lead acetate → black PbS); N\nand S together → blood-red Fe(SCN)²⁺; halogens: AgNO₃ after boiling with\nHNO₃ (white AgCl, pale-yellow AgBr, yellow AgI); P: oxidise to\nphosphate, ammonium molybdate → yellow ppt.</li>\n<li>Dumas (N as N₂): %N = (28 × V × 100)/(22400 × w); Kjeldahl: %N = 1.4\n× N × V/w (not for nitro, azo, ring N).</li>\n<li>Carius: %Cl = (35.5/143.5)(m_AgCl/m) × 100; %S = (32/233)(m_BaSO₄/m)\n× 100; %C = (12/44)(m_CO₂/m) × 100; %H = (2/18)(m_H₂O/m) × 100.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Learn the formulas as fractions: atomic\nmass/molar mass of the weighed product.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C14": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> IUPAC names (24.73, 26.81), carbocation\nstability (24.76), geometrical isomerism (25.58), cyclic ether isomers\n(25.86), metamers (26.66), benzyl carbocation (R.65), conjugated dienes\n(R.76).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Priority of groups: COOH &gt; SO₃H &gt; COOR &gt; COX &gt; CONH₂\n&gt; CN &gt; CHO &gt; C=O &gt; OH &gt; NH₂ &gt; C=C &gt; C≡C. Longest\nchain with the principal group, lowest locants, alphabetical order.</li>\n<li>Carbocation stability: 3° &gt; 2° &gt; 1° &gt; CH₃⁺; benzyl and\nallyl are resonance-stabilised.</li>\n<li>Inductive effect: −NO₂ &gt; −CN &gt; −F &gt; −Cl &gt; −Br &gt; −I;\n+I: tert-alkyl &gt; iso-propyl &gt; ethyl &gt; methyl. +M: −OH, −OR,\n−NH₂, halogens (weak); −M: −NO₂, −CN, −CHO, −COOH.</li>\n<li>Hyperconjugation counts α-H; more α-H gives a more stable alkene,\ncarbocation or radical.</li>\n<li>Isomerism: chain, position, functional, metamerism (same functional\ngroup, different alkyl groups on either side, e.g. ethers), tautomerism;\ngeometrical needs two different groups on each C of the double bond;\noptical needs a chiral centre (2ⁿ stereoisomers).</li>\n<li>Degree of unsaturation = (2C + 2 + N − H − X)/2. Alkane isomers: C₄\n2, C₅ 3, C₆ 5, C₇ 9.</li>\n<li>Intermediates: carbocation sp² planar; carbanion sp³ pyramidal;\nradical sp² planar.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For a name question, find the principal chain\nfirst, then number to give the principal group the smallest locant.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C15": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> hydroboration–oxidation (24.58), alkane\nisomer b.p. (24.62), KMnO₄ oxidation (24.97), HBr peroxide effect\n(25.49), benzene preparation (25.60), monochlorination count (25.75),\nC–H dissociation (25.76), bromine water test (25.77), benzene\nchlorination (26.51, R.51), alkyne reduction (R.86).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alkanes: more branching → lower boiling point; free-radical\nhalogenation (initiation, propagation, termination); selectivity 3° &gt;\n2° &gt; 1° (Cl: ~5 : 3.8 : 1; Br is far more selective). Staggered\nethane is the most stable conformer. Number of monochlorination products\n= number of distinct hydrogen types.</li>\n<li>Alkenes: HX adds by Markovnikov; peroxide effect only for HBr\n(anti-Markovnikov); hydration Markovnikov; hydroboration–oxidation\nanti-Markovnikov, syn addition. Ozonolysis (O₃, Zn/H₂O) gives\naldehydes/ketones. Cold dilute alkaline KMnO₄ gives cis-diol; hot acidic\ngives acids/ketones/CO₂. Br₂/CCl₄ or Baeyer reagent tests\nunsaturation.</li>\n<li>Alkynes: terminal alkynes are acidic (react with Na, NaNH₂,\nammoniacal AgNO₃ → white ppt, Cu₂Cl₂ → red ppt); Lindlar's catalyst →\ncis-alkene; Na/liquid NH₃ → trans-alkene; hydration (HgSO₄/H₂SO₄) gives\nketone (acetaldehyde from ethyne); acidity from s-character sp &gt; sp²\n&gt; sp³; C–C bond length ethane &gt; ethene &gt; ethyne.</li>\n<li>Benzene: Hückel (4n + 2)π electrons, planar, cyclic, conjugated.\nPreparations: decarboxylation of sodium benzoate, trimerisation of\nethyne (hot iron tube), reduction of phenol with zinc dust. EAS:\nnitration (NO₂⁺), halogenation (X₂ + FeX₃), sulphonation, Friedel–Crafts\nalkylation and acylation (fails on strongly deactivated rings). Benzene\n+ Cl₂ with FeCl₃ → chlorobenzene (substitution); with Cl₂ in sunlight →\nhexachlorocyclohexane (addition) (26.51).</li>\n<li>Directing: o/p (activating) −OH, −NH₂, −OR, −R; o/p (deactivating)\nhalogens; meta: −NO₂, −CN, −CHO, −COR, −COOH, −SO₃H.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Count monochlorination products by listing H\ntypes after drawing symmetry.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C16": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> S_N1 reactivity (24.75), alcohol +\nPBr₃/elimination (24.88), S_N2 leaving group (25.84), PCl₅ (26.52),\nGrignard with an amine (R.52).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S_N1: 3° &gt; 2° &gt; 1°, carbocation, racemisation, polar protic\nsolvent. S_N2: 1° &gt; 2° &gt; 3°, inversion (Walden), polar aprotic\nsolvent. Leaving group I &gt; Br &gt; Cl &gt; F.</li>\n<li>Preparation: alcohol + SOCl₂ (best, gaseous by-products), PCl₅,\nPCl₃, HX/ZnCl₂; Finkelstein (NaI/acetone → R–I); Swarts (AgF → R–F);\nSandmeyer (CuCl/HCl, CuBr/HBr); Gattermann (Cu powder).</li>\n<li>Haloarenes are resistant to nucleophilic substitution; −NO₂ at o/p\npositions activates (2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene).</li>\n<li>Elimination (alcoholic KOH, Saytzeff) vs substitution (aqueous\nKOH).</li>\n<li>Grignard RMgX in dry ether reacts with active H (water, alcohol,\namine → alkane).</li>\n<li>Chloroform oxidises in air and light to phosgene (store in dark,\nfull bottles); iodoform is an antiseptic; freons deplete ozone; DDT is\npersistent.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Alkyl halide + AgNO₃ (aq): the order of\nprecipitate speed follows carbocation stability.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C17": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> reagent matches (24.59), Lucas test\n(24.61), Fehling (24.66), alcohol + PCl₃/PCl₅ (24.100), carboxylic acid\nacidity (25.70), Grignard on nitrile (25.78), DIBAL (25.83), alcohol\npreparation (26.46), 2,4-DNP and oxidation (26.72), Etard (R.46),\np-nitrotoluene products (R.57), crossed aldol (R.60), polarity (R.71),\nhaloform on acetophenone (R.90).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol preparation: hydroboration, hydration, Grignard + HCHO (1°),\n+ other aldehydes (2°), + ketones (3°); LiAlH₄ reduces aldehydes,\nketones, acids, esters; NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes and ketones only.</li>\n<li>Lucas (ZnCl₂/conc. HCl): 3° immediate turbidity; 2° in ~5 min; 1°\nnone at room temperature.</li>\n<li>Oxidation: PCC stops at aldehyde (1°); K₂Cr₂O₇/KMnO₄ gives acid; 2°\n→ ketone; 3° resists. Dehydration: conc. H₂SO₄ at 443 K → alkene; 413 K\n→ ether (1°); ease 3° &gt; 2° &gt; 1°. Acidity: 1° &gt; 2° &gt; 3°;\nphenol is more acidic than alcohol.</li>\n<li>Phenol acidity rises with EWG: picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol)\n&gt; p-nitrophenol &gt; m-nitrophenol &gt; phenol &gt; cresols.\nReactions: Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH → salicylaldehyde), Kolbe\n(CO₂/NaOH → salicylic acid), Br₂ water → 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white\nppt), neutral FeCl₃ → violet.</li>\n<li>Williamson ether: RONa + R′X (S_N2, use a primary halide).</li>\n<li>Aldehydes/ketones, preparation: Rosenmund (RCOCl, H₂/Pd–BaSO₄),\nEtard (toluene + CrO₂Cl₂ → benzaldehyde, R.46), Stephen (RCN +\nSnCl₂/HCl), DIBAL-H (esters/nitriles at low temperature, 25.83),\nGattermann–Koch (benzene + CO/HCl/AlCl₃), Friedel–Crafts acylation,\nGrignard + nitrile → ketone (25.78), Cu/573 K dehydrogenation of 1°\nalcohols.</li>\n<li>Nucleophilic addition: HCN (cyanohydrin), NaHSO₃, NH₂OH (oxime),\nNH₂NH₂ (hydrazone), 2,4-DNP (orange-red ppt; test for C=O). Reactivity\nHCHO &gt; RCHO &gt; ArCHO &gt; ketones.</li>\n<li>Aldol needs α-H (dil. NaOH); Cannizzaro (no α-H: HCHO, benzaldehyde;\nconc. alkali) → alcohol + acid salt; haloform (NaOH + I₂) for CH₃CO–R\nand CH₃CH(OH)R → yellow iodoform (acetophenone gives it, R.90).</li>\n<li>Reduction: Clemmensen (Zn–Hg/HCl; not for acid-sensitive),\nWolff–Kishner (NH₂NH₂/KOH; not for base-sensitive).</li>\n<li>Tests: Tollens (silver mirror) – all aldehydes; Fehling (red Cu₂O) –\naliphatic aldehydes only, not aromatic aldehydes or ketones (24.66);\nformic acid reduces both.</li>\n<li>Carboxylic acid acidity: HCOOH &gt; CH₃COOH; Cl₃CCOOH &gt; Cl₂CHCOOH\n&gt; ClCH₂COOH &gt; CH₃COOH; benzoic acid (pKₐ 4.2) stronger than acetic\n(4.76); EDG weakens, EWG strengthens (p-nitrobenzoic strongest).\nReactions: HVZ α-halogenation, esterification, NaHCO₃ effervescence\n(distinguishes acids from phenols), SOCl₂ → acyl chloride, LiAlH₄ →\nalcohol, soda-lime decarboxylation.</li>\n<li>Boiling points (similar M): acids &gt; alcohols &gt;\naldehydes/ketones &gt; ethers &gt; alkanes.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Name reactions: list each with its reagent in\none line and revise daily.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C18": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> aniline Friedel–Crafts and Gabriel\n(24.63), nitrile → amide (Hoffmann) (24.95), amine basic strength\n(25.67), diazonium preparation (25.74), ethylamine + nitrous acid\n(26.47), nitrile reduction (26.61), isocyanide (26.89), aniline\nreactions (R.58).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Basic strength in water: (C₂H₅)₂NH &gt; (C₂H₅)₃N &gt; C₂H₅NH₂ &gt;\nNH₃; methyl series (CH₃)₂NH &gt; CH₃NH₂ &gt; (CH₃)₃N &gt; NH₃; aryl\namines are weaker (aniline &lt; NH₃; p-toluidine &gt; aniline &gt;\np-nitroaniline). Gas phase: 3° &gt; 2° &gt; 1°.</li>\n<li>Preparation: Gabriel phthalimide (1° aliphatic only); Hoffmann\nbromamide (RCONH₂ + Br₂/NaOH → RNH₂, one C fewer); nitrile reduction\n(RCN → RCH₂NH₂); nitro reduction (Sn/HCl, Fe/HCl, H₂/Pd);\nammonolysis.</li>\n<li>Hinsberg (C₆H₅SO₂Cl): 1° → sulphonamide soluble in alkali; 2° →\ninsoluble; 3° → no reaction. Carbylamine (CHCl₃ + KOH): only 1° amines,\nfoul-smelling isocyanide (26.89).</li>\n<li>Nitrous acid: aliphatic 1° → alcohol + N₂; aromatic 1° at 273–278 K\n→ diazonium salt; 2° → N-nitrosoamine (yellow oil).</li>\n<li>Diazonium: Sandmeyer (CuCl, CuBr, CuCN), Gattermann (Cu powder), I\nvia KI (no Cu), F via HBF₄, H via H₃PO₂, OH via warm water; coupling\nwith phenol (pH 9–10) and aniline (pH 4–5) → azo dyes at the para\nposition. Aniline + NaNO₂ + HCl at 0–5 °C → benzenediazonium chloride\n(25.74).</li>\n<li>Friedel–Crafts fails on aniline (amine reacts with AlCl₃); acetylate\nfirst to control substitution (24.63). Aniline + bromine water →\n2,4,6-tribromoaniline.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For \"amine order\" questions, check solvent:\nwater (2° &gt; 3°) or gas phase (3° &gt; 2°).</li>\n</ul>\n", "C19": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> glucose reactions (24.51), vitamin\ndeficiency diseases (25.69, R.89), fructose (25.82), DNA/RNA structure\n(26.75), peptides and proteins (R.56).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucose: aldohexose; HI → n-hexane (linear chain); Br₂ water →\ngluconic acid; HNO₃ → saccharic acid; α-D-(+)-glucose and β-form are\nanomers (differ at C-1); mutarotation. Fructose is a ketohexose.</li>\n<li>Sucrose is non-reducing (C-1 α-glucose to C-2 β-fructose);\nhydrolysis gives invert sugar. Maltose (α-1,4 glucose) and lactose\n(β-1,4 galactose + glucose) are reducing.</li>\n<li>Starch: amylose (α-1,4, ~20%) + amylopectin (α-1,4 and α-1,6, ~80%);\ncellulose is β-1,4; glycogen resembles amylopectin.</li>\n<li>Proteins: peptide bond (–CONH–); primary sequence, secondary\n(α-helix, β-sheet, hydrogen bonds), tertiary folding, quaternary (e.g.\nhaemoglobin α₂β₂). Denaturation (heat, pH) breaks secondary and tertiary\nstructure, not the primary.</li>\n<li>Vitamins: A (night blindness, xerophthalmia), B₁ (beri-beri), B₂\n(cheilosis), B₆ (convulsions), B₁₂ (pernicious anaemia), C (scurvy), D\n(rickets, osteomalacia), E (RBC fragility), K (poor clotting), biotin\n(dermatitis). Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K; water-soluble: B group and\nC.</li>\n<li>Nucleic acids: nucleoside = sugar + base; nucleotide = + phosphate;\nDNA has deoxyribose and A, G, C, T; RNA has ribose and A, G, C, U; A=T\n(2 H-bonds), G≡C (3 H-bonds).</li>\n<li>Hormones: insulin (polypeptide), thyroxine (amino-acid derivative),\nestradiol (steroid).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Vitamin–disease pairs: learn as a two-column\ntable and recite.</li>\n</ul>\n", "C20": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> cation group analysis (24.91, 25.48),\nMohr's salt preparation (24.94), acetate test (26.60), phenolphthalein\nNaOH–oxalic acid titration (26.68), phthalein dye test (26.74),\nKMnO₄–oxalic acid titration (R.74).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cation groups:</strong> I Pb²⁺ (dil. HCl, white PbCl₂); II\nCu²⁺ (H₂S in dil. HCl, black CuS); III Al³⁺ (white gelatinous), Fe³⁺\n(reddish brown) (NH₄Cl + NH₄OH); IV Zn²⁺ (white ZnS), Ni²⁺ (black NiS)\n(H₂S in ammoniacal medium); V Ca²⁺, Ba²⁺ ((NH₄)₂CO₃, white carbonates);\nVI Mg²⁺ (Na₂HPO₄ → white ppt). NH₄⁺: Nessler's reagent (brown) or NaOH +\nheat → NH₃.</li>\n<li><strong>Anions:</strong> CO₃²⁻ (dil. HCl, CO₂ turns lime water\nmilky); S²⁻ (H₂S, lead acetate paper black; nitroprusside violet); SO₃²⁻\n(SO₂, turns acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ green); NO₂⁻ (brown fumes, starch-iodide\nblue); NO₃⁻ (brown ring test with FeSO₄ and conc. H₂SO₄); Cl⁻ (AgNO₃\nwhite curdy ppt, soluble in NH₄OH; chromyl chloride test red vapours);\nBr⁻ (pale-yellow ppt, partially soluble; CS₂ layer orange-brown); I⁻\n(yellow ppt, insoluble; CS₂ layer violet). Acetate: vinegar smell with\ndil. H₂SO₄, neutral FeCl₃ red colour, fruity ester smell with ethanol +\nconc. H₂SO₄ (26.60). The OCR of the official list may have dropped\nacetate; the 2026 paper tested it, so include it.</li>\n<li><strong>Titrations:</strong> KMnO₄ vs oxalic acid in dilute H₂SO₄ at\n~60–70 °C, self-indicator, permanent light pink end point, never HCl or\nHNO₃; 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O. Mohr's salt\nFeSO₄·(NH₄)₂SO₄·6H₂O vs KMnO₄ (Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺). Phenolphthalein (8.3–10)\nfor weak acid vs strong base (oxalic acid vs NaOH); methyl orange\n(3.1–4.4) for strong acid vs weak base.</li>\n<li><strong>Preparations:</strong> Mohr's salt (equimolar FeSO₄ and\n(NH₄)₂SO₄ in dilute H₂SO₄, crystallise); potash alum (K₂SO₄ +\nAl₂(SO₄)₃); acetanilide (aniline + acetic anhydride); p-nitroacetanilide\n(nitration of acetanilide, para major); aniline yellow (diazonium +\naniline); iodoform (ethanol or acetone + I₂/NaOH).</li>\n<li>Phthalein test for phenol: phenol + phthalic anhydride + conc. H₂SO₄\n→ phenolphthalein, pink in alkali (26.74).</li>\n<li>Experiments: enthalpy of solution of CuSO₄; enthalpy of\nneutralisation of strong acid and strong base (≈ −57 kJ/mol); lyophilic\n(starch, gelatin) and lyophobic (Fe(OH)₃, As₂S₃) sols; I⁻ + H₂O₂ kinetic\nstudy.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Group-separation order: \"Pb, Cu, Al-Fe, Zn-Ni,\nCa-Ba, Mg\" (I to VI).</li>\n</ul>\n", "B1": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> fungi classification (24.115, 24.118,\nR.96), Phaeophyceae (24.136), animal phyla and fish examples (24.161,\n24.166), coelom types (24.169, 25.109), pteridophyte and bryophyte life\ncycles (25.111, 25.119, 25.168), Whittaker kingdoms (25.132, 26.121),\nCyclostomata (25.139, 26.142), gymnosperms (25.159, 26.100, R.94),\nechinoderms (25.166, R.133), binomial rules (26.131), penguin (26.150),\nOsteichthyes (26.153), prions (R.99), taxonomic hierarchy (R.116,\nR.124), lichens (R.128), Reptilia (R.91).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hierarchy: species → genus → family → order → class →\nphylum/division → kingdom. <strong>[T]</strong> \"Kids Prefer Cheese Over\nFried Green Spinach\" (K P C O F G S reversed).</li>\n<li>Binomial nomenclature (Linnaeus): genus capitalised, species lower\ncase, both italic or underlined separately; author name after the\nspecies.</li>\n<li>Whittaker (1969) five kingdoms on cell structure, body organisation,\nnutrition, reproduction, phylogeny: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae,\nAnimalia.</li>\n<li>Viruses (nucleic acid + capsid, obligate parasites), viroids (free\nRNA, e.g. PSTV), prions (protein, e.g. BSE/mad cow); lichens = alga +\nfungus mutualism, pollution (SO₂) indicators.</li>\n<li>Fungi: Phycomycetes (Mucor, Rhizopus; coenocytic), Ascomycetes\n(Penicillium, Aspergillus, Neurospora; conidia and ascospores),\nBasidiomycetes (Agaricus, Ustilago, Puccinia), Deuteromycetes\n(Alternaria, Trichoderma). Plasmogamy → dikaryophase → karyogamy\n(R.96).</li>\n<li>Algae: Chlorophyceae (chl a, b; starch; Chlamydomonas, Volvox,\nSpirogyra, Chara); Phaeophyceae (chl a, c, fucoxanthin; mannitol,\nlaminarin; Laminaria, Fucus); Rhodophyceae (chl a, d; phycoerythrin;\nfloridean starch; Polysiphonia, Porphyra, Gracilaria and Gelidium for\nagar; Chondrus for carrageen).</li>\n<li>Bryophytes: gametophyte dominant; gemmae in Marchantia (25.168);\nmoss protonema. Pteridophytes: sporophyte dominant; classes Psilopsida\n(Psilotum), Lycopsida (Selaginella, Lycopodium), Sphenopsida\n(Equisetum), Pteropsida (ferns); heterospory in Selaginella and\nSalvinia. Gymnosperms: naked ovules; Cycas, Pinus, Ginkgo; coralloid\nroots (Cycas).</li>\n<li>Animals: Porifera (cellular level, choanocytes), Cnidaria\n(diploblastic, cnidoblasts), Ctenophora (comb plates), Platyhelminthes\n(acoelomate, flame cells), Aschelminthes (pseudocoelomate), Annelida\n(metameric, true coelom), Arthropoda (largest phylum, chitin), Mollusca\n(mantle, radula), Echinodermata (water vascular system; adult radial,\nlarva bilateral), Hemichordata, Chordata.</li>\n<li>Classes: Cyclostomata (jawless, no scales; Petromyzon, Myxine);\nChondrichthyes (cartilaginous, placoid scales, no air bladder;\nScoliodon, Pristis, Trygon); Osteichthyes (bony, air bladder; Labeo,\nCatla, Hippocampus, Exocoetus); Reptilia (dry scaly skin; Chelone,\nNaja); Aves (feathers, pneumatic bones; Aptenodytes penguin); Mammalia\n(mammary glands; Ornithorhynchus lays eggs; Macropus pouch; Pteropus;\nBalaenoptera; Delphinus).</li>\n<li>Heart chambers: fishes 2; amphibians 3; most reptiles 3 (crocodile\n4); birds 4; mammals 4.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For each animal group, learn one salient\nfeature and two examples; NEET always asks one of the two.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B2": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> guard cells, bulliform cells (24.111,\n24.134, 26.107), flower types (24.113, 24.114, 25.155), seed structure\n(24.128, 25.124, 25.141, R.145), stamens (24.144), muscle and epithelial\ntissue (24.162, 24.186), cockroach (24.172, 24.193), frog (25.94,\n25.107, 25.169, 25.175, 26.164, 26.166, R.108, R.135), floral formula\n(25.104, 26.133, R.132), monocot stem (25.118), placentation (26.101,\nR.127), root hair zone (26.102), plant tissues (26.109), racemose\ninflorescence (26.112), phyllotaxy (R.98).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Root regions: root cap, meristematic, elongation, maturation (root\nhairs) (26.102).</li>\n<li>Phyllotaxy: alternate (China rose, mustard, sunflower), opposite\n(Calotropis, guava), whorled (Alstonia).</li>\n<li>Inflorescence: racemose (unlimited, acropetal) vs cymose (limited,\nbasipetal). Flower position: hypogynous (superior ovary: mustard, China\nrose, brinjal), perigynous (rose, plum, peach), epigynous (inferior\novary: guava, cucumber, sunflower).</li>\n<li>Placentation: marginal (pea), axile (China rose, tomato, lemon),\nparietal (mustard, Argemone), free central (Dianthus, primrose), basal\n(sunflower, marigold).</li>\n<li>Families: Malvaceae, Cruciferae/Brassicaceae, Leguminosae/Fabaceae,\nCompositae/Asteraceae, Gramineae/Poaceae are in the syllabus;\n<strong>NEET 2026 and Re-NEET also asked Solanaceae</strong> (26.133,\nR.132), so learn Solanaceae and Liliaceae too. Floral formulas:\nSolanaceae ⊕ ⚥ K(5) C(5) A5 G(2); Fabaceae % ⚥ K(5) C1+2+(2) A(9)+1 G1;\nBrassicaceae ⊕ ⚥ K2+2 C4 A2+4 G(2); Malvaceae ⊕ ⚥ K(5) C5 A(∞) G(5);\nLiliaceae ⊕ ⚥ P(3+3) A3+3 G(3).</li>\n<li>Seed: dicot (bean: testa, tegmen, hilum, micropyle, 2 cotyledons,\nplumule, radicle); monocot (maize: scutellum = single cotyledon,\ncoleoptile, coleorhiza, aleurone layer, endosperm). Non-albuminous seeds\n(pea, groundnut, bean) have no residual endosperm (R.145).</li>\n<li>Tissues: meristematic (apical, intercalary, lateral); simple\npermanent (parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma); complex (xylem,\nphloem). Dicot stem: vascular bundles in a ring, conjoint, collateral,\nopen, hypodermis collenchymatous; monocot stem: scattered, closed,\nsclerenchymatous hypodermis, bundle sheath (25.118). Dorsiventral leaf\n(palisade + spongy) vs isobilateral (monocot, bulliform cells; guard\ncells dumb-bell in grasses, kidney in dicots) (24.111, 26.107).</li>\n<li>Animal tissues: simple squamous (alveoli, blood vessels), cuboidal\n(PCT with brush border, gland ducts), columnar (stomach, intestine),\nciliated (bronchioles, fallopian tubes), compound (skin). Muscle:\nskeletal (striated, voluntary, multinucleate), smooth (spindle,\ninvoluntary, no striations), cardiac (striated, involuntary,\nintercalated discs). Junctions: tight, adhering, gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Frog (Rana tigrina):</strong> moist skin (cutaneous\nrespiration); 3-chambered heart (2 atria, 1 ventricle) with sinus\nvenosus and truncus arteriosus; renal portal and hepatic portal systems;\nnucleated RBCs; kidneys mesonephric, ureotelic; 10 pairs of cranial\nnerves; male has vocal sacs and a copulatory pad on the first digit of\nthe forelimb; external fertilisation.</li>\n<li><strong>Cockroach (Periplaneta americana)</strong> — the official\ntext prints \"insect (Frog)\", so confirm with your teacher; NEET asked\ncockroach in 2019–2024 (24.172, 24.193): 10 abdominal segments;\nforewings = tegmina (mesothorax), hindwings membranous; anal cerci in\nboth sexes, anal styles only in males; crop, gizzard, hepatic caecae,\nMalpighian tubules; open circulation with 13-chambered heart; tracheal\nrespiration through 10 pairs of spiracles; nervous system 3 thoracic and\n6 abdominal ganglia on a ventral nerve cord.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Floral formula: write symbol-by-symbol: ⊕\nregular, % zygomorphic, ⚥ bisexual; G̲ superior, Ḡ inferior ovary.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B3": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> lecithin (24.102), competitive\ninhibition (24.119), organelles (24.127, 25.121, 26.152, R.112),\nkinetochore (24.129), meiosis prophase I (24.130, 24.163, R.126),\ncofactor and apoenzyme (24.135, 25.149), chloroplast DNA (24.139),\nGLUT-4/insulin/trypsin/collagen (24.146), enzyme bond types (24.156),\ncell cycle (24.171, 26.93, R.134), enzyme classes (25.117, 26.176),\nribosomes (25.98, 26.148), mesosome (25.128), haem enzyme (25.131),\nnucleotide (25.137), Golgi (25.140), mitosis (25.158), nucleolus\n(26.92), protein structure (26.105, 26.106), cell membrane (26.163),\nmitochondria (R.97, 24.199), cell theory (R.100), chromoplast (R.107),\nendomembrane (R.120).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prokaryotes: 70S ribosomes (50S + 30S), nucleoid, plasmid, mesosome,\nno membrane-bound organelles. Eukaryotes: 80S (60S + 40S); mitochondria\nand chloroplasts have 70S ribosomes and circular DNA (24.139).</li>\n<li>Endomembrane system = ER, Golgi, lysosomes, vacuoles only (not\nmitochondria, chloroplasts or peroxisomes) (R.120). Non-membranous:\nribosomes, nucleolus, centrioles, cytoskeleton (26.148).</li>\n<li>Nucleolus: rRNA synthesis and ribosome assembly (26.92). Golgi:\npackaging, glycosylation, secretion. Lysosomes: hydrolytic enzymes,\nacidic pH. RER: protein synthesis; SER: lipids, steroids,\ndetoxification.</li>\n<li>Chloroplast: grana (light reaction), stroma (dark reaction).\nMitochondria: matrix (TCA enzymes), inner membrane (ETS, F₀F₁).\nPlastids: chromoplast (carotenoids, xanthophyll), leucoplast\n(amyloplast, elaioplast, aleuroplast).</li>\n<li>Cell membrane: fluid mosaic model (Singer and Nicolson 1972); cell\ntheory: Schleiden and Schwann; Virchow \"omnis cellula e cellula\".</li>\n<li>Biomolecules: phospholipid lecithin (phosphatidylcholine); protein\nlevels primary (peptide bond), secondary (α-helix, β-sheet), tertiary,\nquaternary (haemoglobin α₂β₂); collagen is the most abundant animal\nprotein; RuBisCO the most abundant on Earth. Polysaccharides: starch\n(helical), cellulose (no helical structure), glycogen, chitin\n(N-acetylglucosamine). Nucleotide = base + sugar + phosphate; bonds:\nN-glycosidic (base–sugar), phosphoester (sugar–phosphate),\nphosphodiester (between nucleotides).</li>\n<li>Enzymes: six classes in order: oxidoreductases, transferases,\nhydrolases, lyases, isomerases, ligases. <strong>[T]</strong> \"Only The\nHungry Lion Is Lazy\". Apoenzyme + cofactor = holoenzyme; prosthetic\ngroup (haem in catalase), coenzyme (NAD, NADP, vitamin derivatives),\nmetal ion. Competitive inhibitor resembles the substrate (Km rises, Vmax\nunchanged), e.g. malonate vs succinate dehydrogenase. Km = substrate\nconcentration at ½Vmax. Low temperature inactivates reversibly; high\ntemperature denatures.</li>\n<li>Secondary metabolites: pigments (carotenoids, anthocyanins),\nalkaloids (morphine, codeine), terpenoids, essential oils, toxins\n(abrin, ricin), lectins (concanavalin A), drugs (vinblastine, curcumin),\npolymeric substances (rubber, gums).</li>\n<li>Cell cycle: G₁, S (DNA replication, 2C → 4C), G₂, M; G₀ quiescent.\nMitosis: prophase (condensation), metaphase (chromosomes at the equator,\nspindle attached to kinetochores), anaphase (centromere splits),\ntelophase; cytokinesis by furrow (animals) or cell plate (plants).</li>\n<li>Meiosis I prophase I: leptotene, zygotene (synapsis, synaptonemal\ncomplex), pachytene (crossing over), diplotene (chiasmata; oocytes\narrest here), diakinesis (terminalisation, nucleolus disappears).\n<strong>[T]</strong> \"Lazy Zebras Prefer Dull Days\". Meiosis I is\nreductional, II equational.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> For every organelle, one line: structure,\nfunction, one disease or one unusual fact.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B4": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> auxin as weedicide (24.101, R.105), dark\nreaction (24.107), dedifferentiation (24.120), Calvin cycle ATP/NADPH\n(24.124, 26.97), photorespiration (24.138, R.93, R.114), respiration\nsites (24.142, 26.135), TCA (24.147), gibberellin (24.148, R.101), ETC\ncomplex II (25.91), cytokinin (25.108), chromatogram (25.122), RuBisCO\n(25.171, 26.132), growth (25.173, 26.103, R.121), heterophylly (26.111),\nPGR match (26.126), RQ (26.128), glycolysis (R.95), P700 (R.102).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Photosynthesis: O₂ comes from water (van Niel; Ruben isotope);\nchlorophyll a is the main pigment; chromatogram from top: carotene\n(yellow-orange, travels farthest), xanthophyll (yellow), chlorophyll a\n(blue-green), chlorophyll b (yellow-green) (25.122).</li>\n<li>Light reaction: PSII (P680, grana) photolysis of water in the lumen;\nPSI (P700, stroma lamellae). Non-cyclic gives ATP, NADPH, O₂; cyclic\n(PSI only) gives ATP only. Chemiosmosis: proton gradient across the\nthylakoid, ATP synthase CF₀–CF₁, protons accumulate in the lumen.</li>\n<li>Calvin cycle (stroma): carboxylation (RuBisCO: RuBP + CO₂ → 2 PGA),\nreduction, regeneration. Per CO₂: 3 ATP + 2 NADPH; per glucose 18 ATP +\n12 NADPH, 6 turns. C₄ pathway: Kranz anatomy, PEP carboxylase in\nmesophyll → OAA → bundle sheath CO₂; no photorespiration, higher\ntemperature optimum (C₄ 30–40 °C, C₃ 20–25 °C); maize, sugarcane,\nsorghum.</li>\n<li>Photorespiration: RuBisCO oxygenase; chloroplast → peroxisome →\nmitochondrion; no ATP/NADPH made; loses CO₂ (R.93).</li>\n<li>Respiration: glycolysis in cytoplasm (net 2 ATP + 2 NADH, 2\npyruvate); fermentation (yeast: ethanol + CO₂; muscle: lactic acid); TCA\nin matrix: per turn 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 GTP/ATP, 2 CO₂; succinate\ndehydrogenase is Complex II on the inner membrane (25.91). ETS: Complex\nI (NADH dehydrogenase), II (SDH), III (cyt bc₁), IV (cytochrome c\noxidase), O₂ final acceptor; NCERT counts 38 ATP per glucose\n(aerobic).</li>\n<li>RQ = CO₂/O₂: carbohydrates 1; fats 0.7; proteins 0.9; anaerobic\n∞.</li>\n<li>Growth: phases meristematic, elongation, maturation; arithmetic vs\ngeometric growth (R.121); differentiation, dedifferentiation (cambium),\nredifferentiation; plasticity (heterophylly in cotton, coriander,\nlarkspur) (26.111).</li>\n<li>PGRs: <strong>Auxin</strong> (IAA, IBA, NAA; 2,4-D herbicide for\ndicots; apical dominance, root initiation, parthenocarpy in tomato,\nprevents leaf/fruit drop); <strong>Gibberellin</strong> (internode\nelongation, bolting, sugarcane yield, seedless fruit, malting, conifer\nmaturity); <strong>Cytokinin</strong> (cell division, zeatin, delays\nsenescence, breaks apical dominance); <strong>Ethylene</strong> (gas;\nfruit ripening, female flowers in cucumber, rapid internode growth in\ndeep-water rice, breaks dormancy); <strong>ABA</strong> (stress hormone;\ncloses stomata, seed dormancy; antagonist of GA).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> PGR match questions: one word each: auxin =\napical dominance/herbicide, GA = stem, cytokinin = divide, ethylene =\nripen, ABA = stress.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B5": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> brain (24.151, 24.197), lung volumes\n(24.154), nephron (24.170, 24.187, 25.125, R.167), joints (24.173),\nsteroid hormones (24.174), cardiac conduction (24.175, R.123),\noxyhaemoglobin (24.185), ECG (24.189), endocrine disorders (24.192),\ndisorders match (25.101), cardiac regulation (25.112), hormone matches\n(25.148, 25.178, 26.169, R.177), adrenal medulla (25.160), hypothalamic\nhormones (25.165), renin–angiotensin (26.145), respiratory volumes\n(26.146), WBC (26.155), skeletal disorders (26.158), respiration\n(26.160, 26.177), Rh (26.167), skeleton (26.168, R.104, R.119, R.169),\nsynapse (26.172), muscle contraction (26.173, R.163), JGA (26.175),\npituitary (R.113), clotting (R.115), meninges (R.125), pancreas\n(R.131).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lung volumes (mL):</strong> TV 500; IRV 2500–3000; ERV\n1000–1100; RV 1100–1200; IC = TV + IRV; EC = TV + ERV; FRC = ERV + RV;\nVC = ERV + TV + IRV; TLC = VC + RV (~5800). Ventilation = TV ×\nrate.</li>\n<li><strong>Gas transport:</strong> CO₂ ~70% as bicarbonate (carbonic\nanhydrase in RBC), 20–25% carbamino-haemoglobin, ~7% dissolved; O₂ ~97%\non Hb; Hb binds 4 O₂. Sigmoid dissociation curve shifts\n<strong>right</strong> (less affinity) with ↑pCO₂, ↑H⁺ (↓pH),\n↑temperature. Alveolar pO₂ 104, pCO₂ 40 mmHg. Rhythm centre in the\nmedulla; pneumotaxic centre in the pons; chemosensitive area next to the\nrhythm centre senses CO₂ and H⁺.</li>\n<li>Disorders: asthma (bronchial inflammation), emphysema (damaged\nalveolar walls, smoking), occupational (silicosis, asbestosis).</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> plasma 55%; RBC 5–5.5 million/mm³ (no\nnucleus in mammals, ~120 days, spleen \"graveyard\"); WBC 6000–8000/mm³:\nneutrophils 60–65% (most abundant, phagocytic), lymphocytes 20–25%,\nmonocytes 6–8% (largest), eosinophils 2–3%, basophils 0.5–1% (histamine,\nheparin). <strong>[T]</strong> \"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\".\nPlatelets 1.5–3.5 lakh/mm³. Clotting: thromboplastin → prothrombin →\nthrombin (needs Ca²⁺) → fibrinogen → fibrin threads (R.115).</li>\n<li>ABO: A has anti-B; B has anti-A; AB none (universal recipient); O\nboth (universal donor). Rh: Rh⁻ mother with Rh⁺ foetus →\nerythroblastosis foetalis in the second pregnancy; give anti-D after the\nfirst delivery (26.167).</li>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> SAN (pacemaker) 70–75 per minute → AVN →\nbundle of His → Purkinje fibres; cycle 0.8 s (atrial systole 0.1,\nventricular systole 0.3, joint diastole 0.4); lub = closure of AV\nvalves, dub = closure of semilunar valves; cardiac output = stroke\nvolume × heart rate (≈ 70 mL × 72 ≈ 5 L/min). ECG: P = atrial\ndepolarisation; QRS = ventricular depolarisation (start of systole); T =\nventricular repolarisation (end of systole). Sympathetic ↑ rate;\nparasympathetic ↓. Hypertension ≥ 140/90; angina = chest pain from poor\nO₂ supply; heart failure = heart not pumping effectively.</li>\n<li><strong>Excretion:</strong> ammonotelic (bony fish, aquatic\namphibians), ureotelic (mammals, terrestrial amphibians), uricotelic\n(birds, reptiles, insects). Nephron: PCT (brush border, reabsorbs nearly\nall glucose and amino acids, 70–80% of water and electrolytes), loop of\nHenle (descending permeable to water, ascending impermeable to water),\nDCT (conditional reabsorption, secretes H⁺, K⁺, NH₃), collecting duct\n(water reabsorption by ADH). Medullary osmolarity rises from 300 to 1200\nmOsm/L. Juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%, long loop, vasa recta)\nconcentrate urine (24.187). GFR 125 mL/min. JGA (JG cells + macula\ndensa) → renin → angiotensinogen → angiotensin I → II (vasoconstriction\n↑GFR) → aldosterone (Na⁺, water reabsorption); ANF from atria opposes\nthis (26.145). ADH from the neurohypophysis; deficiency causes diabetes\ninsipidus. Disorders: uraemia, renal calculi, glomerulonephritis;\ndialysis (cellophane, heparin).</li>\n<li><strong>Locomotion:</strong> sarcomere Z to Z; A band (myosin,\nconstant), I band (actin, shortens), H zone (myosin only, disappears).\nContraction: ACh at the neuromuscular junction → Ca²⁺ from sarcoplasmic\nreticulum → binds troponin → tropomyosin moves → myosin head\ncross-bridges to actin, using ATP. Red fibres (myoglobin, aerobic) vs\nwhite fibres (anaerobic). Disorders: myasthenia gravis (autoimmune,\nneuromuscular junction), muscular dystrophy (genetic), tetany (low\nCa²⁺), arthritis, osteoporosis (↓ bone mass, ↓ oestrogen), gout (uric\nacid crystals).</li>\n<li>Skeleton: 206 bones; axial 80, appendicular 126; vertebral column 26\nvertebrae (cervical 7, thoracic 12, lumbar 5, sacrum 1 (fused 5), coccyx\n1 (fused 4)); ribs 7 true, 3 false, 2 floating pairs. Joints: fibrous\n(skull sutures), cartilaginous (vertebrae), synovial: ball-and-socket\n(shoulder, hip), hinge (knee, elbow), pivot (atlas–axis), gliding\n(carpals), saddle (thumb).</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> resting potential from K⁺ leak and the\nNa⁺/K⁺ pump (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in); action potential: Na⁺ influx then K⁺\nefflux; synapses electrical (rare, gap junction) or chemical\n(neurotransmitter, ACh). Forebrain: cerebrum (hemispheres joined by\ncorpus callosum), thalamus (relay), hypothalamus (temperature, hunger,\nthirst, hormones); midbrain (corpora quadrigemina); hindbrain: pons,\ncerebellum (balance), medulla (heart rate, respiration). Meninges: dura\n(outer), arachnoid, pia (inner).</li>\n<li><strong>Endocrine:</strong> hypothalamus: releasing (GnRH, TRH, CRH,\nGHRH) and inhibiting (somatostatin, dopamine) hormones via the\nhypophyseal portal; anterior pituitary: GH (acromegaly in adults,\ngigantism in children, dwarfism if deficient), prolactin, TSH, ACTH, LH,\nFSH; posterior: oxytocin, vasopressin. Pineal: melatonin. Thyroid: T₃/T₄\n(goitre = iodine deficiency; exophthalmic goitre = hyper; cretinism),\nthyrocalcitonin ↓ Ca²⁺. Parathyroid: PTH ↑ Ca²⁺. Thymus: thymosin.\nAdrenal cortex: cortisol (gluconeogenesis, anti-inflammatory),\naldosterone; Addison's disease (cortex deficiency); medulla: adrenaline\nand noradrenaline (\"emergency\" hormones: ↑ alertness, pupil dilation,\nsweating, heartbeat, glycogenolysis). Pancreas: glucagon (α,\nhyperglycaemic), insulin (β, hypoglycaemic, GLUT-4), somatostatin (δ);\ndiabetes mellitus. Testis: androgens (Leydig); ovary: oestrogen,\nprogesterone. Peptide hormones act via membrane receptors and second\nmessengers (cAMP, IP₃-Ca²⁺); steroid and thyroid hormones act on\nintracellular receptors and gene expression (24.174).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Hormone matching: write the gland once, then\nlist \"excess\" and \"deficiency\" disease.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B6": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> pollination (24.104, 24.145, 25.102,\n26.134), female reproductive tract (24.152), FSH (24.155), hymen\n(24.168), IUD (24.176, 26.147), natural contraception (24.177),\ncolostrum (24.183), spermatogenesis (24.188, 26.143), IVF/ART (25.93,\n26.138, R.137), sperm (25.110), pregnancy (25.123, 26.141), embryo sac\n(25.127, R.155), gametogenesis (25.138), menarche (25.147), tapetum\n(25.153), female gametophyte (25.154), twins (25.176), microsporogenesis\n(26.115), endosperm (26.122, R.171), ovum covering (26.161, R.147), male\nreproductive system (R.149), ovulation hormones (R.150), STDs (R.156),\nplacenta (R.164), anther (R.174).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flowering plants:</strong> anther is tetragonal with 4\nmicrosporangia; wall layers: epidermis, endothecium, middle layers,\ntapetum (innermost, nourishes pollen) (25.153). Pollen: exine\n(sporopollenin) and intine. Embryo sac: 7 cells, 8 nuclei: 3 antipodals\n(chalazal), 2 synergids (micropylar, filiform apparatus), egg, central\ncell with 2 polar nuclei. Pollination: autogamy, geitonogamy, xenogamy\n(26.134). Wind pollination: light non-sticky pollen, feathery stigma,\nsingle ovule, many flowers (maize). Water pollination is rare\n(Vallisneria, Hydrilla; most water plants are pollinated by insects or\nwind) (24.104). Double fertilisation: syngamy → zygote (2n); triple\nfusion → PEN (3n) (26.122, R.171). Ploidy: pollen n; synergid n;\nantipodal n; nucellus 2n; MMC 2n; endosperm 3n. Apomixis (Asteraceae,\ngrasses); polyembryony (citrus, mango); parthenocarpy (banana).</li>\n<li><strong>Human male:</strong> testes in scrotum (2–2.5 °C lower);\nseminiferous tubules (Sertoli cells nourish; Leydig cells outside\nproduce androgens); path: rete testis → vasa efferentia → epididymis →\nvas deferens → ejaculatory duct → urethra; seminal vesicles (fructose,\n~60% of semen), prostate (alkaline), bulbourethral (lubricating mucus).\nSpermatogenesis: spermatogonia (2n) → primary spermatocyte (2n) →\nsecondary spermatocytes (n) → spermatids → sperms. LH acts on Leydig\ncells (androgens); FSH acts on Sertoli cells (spermiogenesis) (24.188).\nSperm: acrosome (enzymes), nucleus, middle piece (mitochondria), tail\n(25.110).</li>\n<li><strong>Human female:</strong> fallopian tube: infundibulum\n(fimbriae) → ampulla (fertilisation) → isthmus; uterus layers\nperimetrium, myometrium, endometrium. Hymen: its presence or absence is\nnot a reliable virginity indicator (24.168). Oogenesis: oogonia form\nonly in foetal life; primary oocytes arrest at prophase I until puberty;\nsecondary oocyte (n) arrested at metaphase II and completes meiosis II\nonly after sperm entry (second polar body).</li>\n<li>Menstrual cycle (28 days): menstrual (1–5), follicular (6–13; FSH\nand oestrogen ↑), ovulation (day 14; LH surge), luteal (15–28; corpus\nluteum, progesterone). Fertilisation → cleavage → morula → blastocyst\n(trophoblast + inner cell mass) → implantation. Placenta hormones: hCG,\nhPL, oestrogens, progesterone, relaxin. Foetus: heart forms in the first\nmonth; limbs and digits by 8 weeks; first movements and hair on head in\nthe 5th month; eyelashes by 24 weeks (26.141). Parturition: foetal\nejection reflex and oxytocin. Colostrum: first milk, rich in IgA\n(24.183).</li>\n<li><strong>Reproductive health:</strong> barriers (condoms, diaphragms,\ncaps); IUDs (non-medicated Lippes loop; copper-releasing CuT, Cu7,\nMultiload 375 reduce sperm motility; hormone-releasing Progestasert,\nLNG-20 make the uterus unsuitable for implantation); natural methods\n(periodic abstinence days 10–17, withdrawal, lactational amenorrhoea up\nto ~6 months); pills (21-day oestrogen–progestogen; Saheli weekly,\nnon-steroidal); surgical (vasectomy, tubectomy); MTP in the first 12\nweeks is safest; amniocentesis (chromosomal disorders; sex determination\nfor misuse is banned). STDs: gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes,\nchlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis B, HIV. ART: IVF\n(zygote or ≤ 8 blastomeres into the fallopian tube = ZIFT; more than 8\ninto the uterus = IUT), GIFT (ovum from a donor into the fallopian\ntube), ICSI (sperm injected into the ovum) (26.138, R.137), AI/IUI.</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Draw the menstrual graph once: LH peak at day\n14, progesterone peak at day 21.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B7": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> terminology and laws (24.103, 24.117,\n24.122), transcription unit (24.110, 26.104), incomplete dominance\n(24.121), lac operon (24.133, 26.125, R.157), molecular scientists\n(24.143), replication (24.150), human evolution (24.157, 26.140, R.143),\ndisorders (24.164, R.180), transcription (24.178, 24.195, 25.174),\nHardy–Weinberg (24.179, R.159), convergent evolution (24.184, 25.151,\n26.174, R.138), ABO (24.194, 26.144, R.142), geological eras (24.196),\nRNA world (25.96), genome (25.106), RNAi/RNA types (25.114),\npost-transcription (25.129), polygenic (25.130), Hershey–Chase (25.134),\nhistones (25.146, 26.123), dihybrid (25.145), pedigree (25.152), triplet\ncode (25.163), sickle cell (26.113, 26.137), DNA fingerprinting\n(26.116), honeybee (26.118, 26.159), XO (26.154), evolution evidence\n(26.179, R.152), Mendel (R.139), RNA Pol II (R.172), forelimbs (R.162),\nselection types (R.175).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mendel:</strong> dominance, segregation, independent\nassortment; monohybrid F₂ 3:1 (genotype 1:2:1); dihybrid 9:3:3:1; test\ncross with recessive; incomplete dominance gives 1:2:1 in both phenotype\nand genotype (snapdragon, Mirabilis); co-dominance (ABO IᴬIᴮ; MN);\nmultiple alleles (Iᴬ, Iᴮ, i; a person carries two); pleiotropy\n(phenylketonuria, sickle cell); polygenic (skin colour, height; 3 gene\npairs → 7 phenotypes, ratio 1:6:15:20:15:6:1).</li>\n<li>ABO genotypes: A = IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi; B = IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi; AB = IᴬIᴮ; O =\nii.</li>\n<li>Chromosomal theory (Sutton and Boveri); linkage (Morgan);\nrecombination frequency ∝ distance (Sturtevant: 1% = 1 map unit).</li>\n<li>Sex determination: XX–XY (humans, Drosophila); XX–XO (grasshopper,\nmale XO) (26.154); ZZ–ZW (birds, female heterogametic); honey bee\nhaplodiploid: queen and workers 2n = 32, drones n = 16 (from\nunfertilised eggs) producing sperm by mitosis (26.118, 26.159).</li>\n<li>Disorders: sex-linked recessive (haemophilia, red–green colour\nblindness); autosomal recessive (sickle cell: GAG → GTG in DNA, Glu →\nVal at position 6 of the β chain (GAG → GUG in mRNA) (26.137);\nthalassaemia: defective globin synthesis; phenylketonuria); chromosomal\n(Down's 21 trisomy 47; Klinefelter's XXY; Turner's XO, 45) (R.180).</li>\n<li><strong>Molecular basis:</strong> Griffith (transformation),\nAvery–MacLeod–McCarty (DNA is the transforming principle), Hershey–Chase\n(³²P DNA, ³⁵S protein) (25.134). DNA: antiparallel, 10 bp per turn,\npitch 3.4 nm, 0.34 nm between base pairs; Chargaff A = T, G = C. Human\nhaploid genome 3 × 10⁹ bp (~2.2 m of DNA); chromosome 1 has the most\ngenes, Y the fewest. Histones are positively charged (lysine, arginine),\nforming an octamer (H2A, H2B, H3, H4 × 2) with ~200 bp per nucleosome\n(25.146, 26.123). Euchromatin is active; heterochromatin is dense and\ninactive.</li>\n<li>Replication: semi-conservative (Meselson–Stahl); DNA polymerase\nworks 5′ → 3′; leading strand continuous, lagging strand discontinuous\n(Okazaki fragments, DNA ligase) (24.150).</li>\n<li>Transcription: template strand 3′ → 5′; RNA matches the coding\nstrand with U for T. Unit = promoter (upstream) + structural gene +\nterminator (24.110, 26.104). Bacterial RNA polymerase with σ factor\n(initiation) and ρ factor (termination) (25.174). Eukaryotes: Pol I\n(rRNAs), Pol II (hnRNA → mRNA), Pol III (tRNA, 5S rRNA, snRNA) (R.172).\nhnRNA processing: splicing, capping (methyl-guanosine at 5′), tailing\n(poly-A at 3′) (25.129).</li>\n<li>Genetic code: triplet; 64 codons, 61 code amino acids, 3 stop (UAA,\nUAG, UGA); AUG start (Met), UGG only Trp; degenerate, non-overlapping,\nnearly universal (25.163). tRNA is the adapter (cloverleaf; L-shaped\ntertiary structure).</li>\n<li><strong>Lac operon</strong> (Jacob and Monod): i gene codes the\nrepressor; z = β-galactosidase; y = permease (increases permeability); a\n= transacetylase; lactose (allolactose) is the inducer, it binds the\nrepressor and frees the operator; polycistronic mRNA (24.133,\n26.125).</li>\n<li>DNA fingerprinting (Jeffreys): VNTR; steps: isolate DNA →\nrestriction digest → gel electrophoresis → Southern blotting →\nhybridisation with labelled VNTR probe → autoradiography (26.116).</li>\n<li><strong>Evolution:</strong> Oparin–Haldane (chemical evolution);\nMiller–Urey 1953 (CH₄, H₂, NH₃, water vapour at 800 °C with electric\ndischarge → amino acids). Evidence: fossils, comparative anatomy,\nbiogeography, biochemistry. Homologous organs (divergent evolution):\nforelimbs of whale, bat, cheetah, human (R.162). Analogous organs\n(convergent): wings of butterfly and bird, eyes of octopus and mammals,\npotato (stem) vs sweet potato (root) (25.151).</li>\n<li>Darwin: variation, struggle for existence, survival of the fittest;\nLamarck: use and disuse; de Vries: mutation (saltation). Selection\ntypes: stabilising (average birth weight), directional (peppered moth,\nDDT or antibiotic resistance), disruptive (both extremes) (R.175).\nAdaptive radiation: Darwin's finches, Australian marsupials.</li>\n<li>Hardy–Weinberg: p² + 2pq + q² = 1; p + q = 1; equilibrium needs no\nmutation, no gene flow, no genetic drift, no recombination, no selection\n(24.179, R.159).</li>\n<li>Eras and humans: Dryopithecus → Ramapithecus → Australopithecus (~2\nmya) → Homo habilis (650–800 cc) → Homo erectus (900 cc) → Neanderthal\n(1400 cc) → Homo sapiens (arose in Africa) (24.157, 26.140, R.143).\nMesozoic ended with dinosaur extinction; Cenozoic is the age of mammals\n(24.196).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Genetics: practise one pedigree problem every\nday for a week; the rest follows.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B8": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> microbial products (24.123, 25.177,\n26.178), autoimmune (24.158), disease causes (24.159, 24.181), drugs\n(24.180, 26.157, R.144), lymphoid organs (24.190, 25.156, R.178),\nbeverages (25.103), streptokinase (25.105, R.148), N₂-fixing organisms\n(25.113), cancer (25.120, 25.135), antibody structure (25.143), innate\nimmunity (25.167), bioactive molecules (26.151), Plasmodium (26.170,\nR.165), colostrum IgA (R.154).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pathogens: viruses (rhinovirus: common cold; dengue and chikungunya\nspread by <em>Aedes</em>; hepatitis B; HIV); bacteria (typhoid:\n<em>Salmonella typhi</em>, Widal test; pneumonia: <em>Streptococcus\npneumoniae</em>); protozoa (malaria: <em>Plasmodium</em>, <em>P.\nfalciparum</em> most dangerous; amoebiasis: <em>Entamoeba</em>);\nhelminths (ascariasis: <em>Ascaris</em>; filariasis:\n<em>Wuchereria</em>, spread by <em>Culex</em>); fungi (ringworm:\n<em>Microsporum</em>, <em>Trichophyton</em>,\n<em>Epidermophyton</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Plasmodium:</em> sporozoites (infective stage) enter via female\n<em>Anopheles</em> → liver (asexual) → RBCs (merozoites; rupture\nreleases haemozoin: fever and chill cycle) → gametocytes → mosquito gut\n(fertilisation) → sporozoites in salivary glands.</li>\n<li>Immunity: innate (skin and mucus barriers, acidic stomach, tears;\ncells: neutrophils, monocytes, NK cells, macrophages; interferons)\n(25.167); acquired (primary slow, secondary strong, with memory);\nhumoral (B cells, antibodies) vs cell-mediated (T cells, graft\nrejection). Antibody = 2 heavy + 2 light chains (H₂L₂); IgG most\nabundant; IgE in allergy; IgA in colostrum. Active immunity (vaccines)\nvs passive (readymade antibodies: antivenom, colostrum). Autoimmune:\nrheumatoid arthritis. Lymphoid organs: primary (bone marrow, thymus),\nsecondary (spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, Peyer's patches, MALT,\nappendix).</li>\n<li>AIDS: HIV is a retrovirus (RNA → DNA by reverse transcriptase) that\ninfects helper T cells and macrophages; spread by sex, blood, needles,\nmother to child; ELISA test; not by touch or sharing food.</li>\n<li>Cancer: loss of contact inhibition; carcinogens: physical (X-rays,\nγ, UV), chemical (tobacco smoke), biological (oncogenic viruses;\nproto-oncogenes). Detection: biopsy, radiography, CT, MRI, antibodies\nagainst tumour antigens. Treatment: surgery, radiation, chemotherapy,\nimmunotherapy (α-interferon).</li>\n<li>Drugs: opioids — morphine from <em>Papaver somniferum</em>\n(sedative, painkiller), heroin = diacetylmorphine (depressant);\ncannabinoids from <em>Cannabis sativa</em> (marijuana, hashish, charas,\nganja); cocaine from <em>Erythroxylum coca</em> (interferes with\ndopamine transport, stimulant); atropine (<em>Atropa belladonna</em>)\nand <em>Datura</em> are hallucinogens.</li>\n<li>Microbes: LAB (<em>Lactobacillus</em>) converts milk to curd; yeast\n(<em>Saccharomyces</em>) in bread and beer; Swiss cheese holes by\n<em>Propionibacterium sharmanii</em> (CO₂); Roquefort ripened by\n<em>Penicillium roqueforti</em>. Beverages without distillation: wine,\nbeer; with distillation: whisky, brandy, rum (25.103). Acids: citric\n(<em>Aspergillus niger</em>), acetic (<em>Acetobacter aceti</em>),\nlactic (<em>Lactobacillus</em>), butyric (<em>Clostridium\nbutylicum</em>). Streptokinase (<em>Streptococcus</em>) clot buster;\ncyclosporin A (<em>Trichoderma polysporum</em>) immunosuppressant;\nstatins (<em>Monascus purpureus</em>) lower cholesterol. Penicillin:\nFleming 1928, Chain and Florey; <em>Penicillium notatum</em>.</li>\n<li>Sewage: primary (physical sedimentation) then secondary (biological:\naerobic flocs, BOD ↓); anaerobic digesters give biogas (methanogens,\n<em>Methanobacterium</em>: CH₄ + CO₂ + H₂ + H₂S). Biocontrol:\n<em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em>, <em>Trichoderma</em>, baculoviruses.\nBiofertilisers: <em>Rhizobium</em> (legume nodules),\n<em>Azospirillum</em> and <em>Azotobacter</em> (free-living),\n<em>Anabaena</em> (with <em>Azolla</em>), <em>Nostoc</em>, mycorrhiza\n(<em>Glomus</em>: phosphorus uptake, drought resistance) (25.113).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Make a table: disease | pathogen | vector or\nroute. Revise weekly.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B9": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> transgene fate (24.106), Bt toxin\n(24.109, R.153), restriction sites (24.112, 26.98), Ti plasmid (24.153),\nbioreactors (24.165, 25.126), pBR322 (24.167, 26.136), gene therapy\n(24.182), PCR (25.92, 26.119, R.179), selection of recombinants (25.115,\n25.179), insulin (25.116, 25.161), gel electrophoresis (25.136, 26.120),\nRNAi (25.144), cloning enzymes (25.172), GMO tools (26.110),\nbioprospecting (26.117), Bt genes (26.156), α-1-antitrypsin (26.162),\nDNA isolation enzymes (R.136), rDNA terms (R.158), plasmids (R.173).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>rDNA (Cohen and Boyer 1972). Restriction enzymes (type II) cut at\npalindromic sequences giving sticky ends: EcoRI recognises GAATTC and\ncuts between G and A; the name is E (genus <em>Escherichia</em>), co\n(species), R (strain RY13), I (order of discovery).</li>\n<li>pBR322: ori, <em>amp</em>ᴿ, <em>tet</em>ᴿ, <em>rop</em>; BamHI/SalI\nsit in <em>tet</em>ᴿ; PstI/PvuI in <em>amp</em>ᴿ. Insertional\ninactivation: foreign DNA at BamHI inactivates <em>tet</em>ᴿ, so\nrecombinants are ampicillin-resistant and tetracycline-sensitive\n(26.136). Blue–white selection (lacZ): recombinants are white\n(25.179).</li>\n<li>DNA isolation: lysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plants), chitinase\n(fungi), RNase, protease; DNA precipitates as threads in chilled ethanol\n(R.136).</li>\n<li>Gel electrophoresis: DNA is negatively charged and moves to the\nanode; smaller fragments move farther; stained with ethidium bromide and\nseen under UV (orange) (25.136, 26.120).</li>\n<li>PCR: denaturation 94 °C → annealing of primers → extension at 72 °C\nwith Taq polymerase (<em>Thermus aquaticus</em>); each cycle doubles\nDNA, giving 2ⁿ copies (26.119, R.179).</li>\n<li>Host entry: Ca²⁺ and heat shock (42 °C) for bacteria;\nmicro-injection (animal cells); gene gun with gold or tungsten particles\n(plants); disarmed <em>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</em> Ti plasmid\n(24.153).</li>\n<li>Bioreactor: stirred tank with sparger (oxygen), foam breaker,\ntemperature and pH control (25.126).</li>\n<li>Bt: <em>cry</em> genes (<em>cryIAc</em>, <em>cryIIAb</em> against\ncotton bollworms; <em>cryIAb</em> against corn borer); protoxin is\nactivated in the alkaline insect gut and makes pores in midgut cells\n(24.109, R.153).</li>\n<li>RNAi: dsRNA silences mRNA; used against <em>Meloidogyne\nincognita</em> (nematode) in tobacco (25.144).</li>\n<li>Insulin: A chain (21 aa) and B chain (30 aa) joined by disulphide\nbonds; pro-insulin has a C peptide removed on maturation; Eli Lilly\n(1983) made the chains separately in <em>E. coli</em>; oral insulin is\ndigested (25.116, 25.161).</li>\n<li>Gene therapy: ADA deficiency (first case 1990; lymphocytes given a\nfunctional ADA gene by a retroviral vector; not a permanent cure).\nMolecular diagnosis: PCR, ELISA, probes. Transgenic animals: Rosie (cow,\nhuman α-lactalbumin enriched milk); α-1-antitrypsin for emphysema\n(26.162). Biosafety: GEAC. Biopiracy (basmati, turmeric, neem),\nbioprospecting (26.117).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Draw pBR322 once with all restriction sites and\nmark which gene each cut site sits in.</li>\n</ul>\n", "B10": "<p><strong>Seen 24–26:</strong> endangered species (24.105), ex situ\n(24.108, 25.99), logistic growth (24.116, 25.180, 26.165, R.168),\nspecies richness (24.126, R.140), biodiversity loss (24.132, 26.91,\n26.95), ecologists (24.137, 25.133), productivity (24.140, 25.100,\n25.150, 26.94), Gause (24.191), commensalism (25.97), mutualism\n(25.157), evil quartet (25.164), energy flow (25.170, R.176),\ndecomposition (26.99), in situ (26.124), sixth extinction (26.129),\nsexual deceit (26.139), pyramids (26.149), interactions (26.180, R.170),\nextinction (R.146), birth/death (R.161), primary consumers (R.166).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Population growth: exponential (J-curve, dN/dt = rN); logistic\n(Verhulst–Pearl, dN/dt = rN(K − N)/K, sigmoid, K = carrying capacity); r\n= b − d (24.116, 25.180).</li>\n<li>Interactions: mutualism (+,+: lichens, mycorrhiza, fig–wasp, orchid\n<em>Ophrys</em>–bee sexual deceit); competition (−,−: Gause's\ncompetitive exclusion; Abingdon tortoise vs goats; resource\npartitioning, MacArthur's warblers); predation (+,−; keystone predator\n<em>Pisaster</em>; prickly pear and <em>Cactoblastis</em>); parasitism\n(+,−; brood parasitism cuckoo–crow); commensalism (+,0: orchid on a\nmango branch (epiphyte), barnacles on a whale, cattle egret); amensalism\n(0,−: <em>Penicillium</em>).</li>\n<li>Ecosystem: NPP = GPP − R; only ~50% of incident solar radiation is\nPAR and 2–10% of PAR is captured; secondary productivity is formed by\nconsumers. Units: g m⁻² yr⁻¹ or kcal m⁻² yr⁻¹. Decomposition:\nfragmentation → leaching → catabolism → humification → mineralisation;\nlignin and chitin slow it, sugars and nitrogen speed it. Energy flow 10%\nlaw (Lindeman). Grazing food chain dominates aquatic ecosystems;\ndetritus food chain dominates terrestrial. Pyramids: number (can be\ninverted: tree–insect–bird), biomass (inverted in the sea), energy\n(always upright) (26.149).</li>\n<li>Biodiversity: three levels (genetic, species, ecosystem); India has\n2.4% of land area and 8.1% of world species; species–area relationship\nlog S = log C + Z log A (Z 0.1–0.2 for small areas, 0.6–1.2 for very\nlarge areas); latitudinal gradient (more species near the equator);\nAmazon rain forest: 40,000 plant species, 3000 fishes, 1300 birds.\nRobert May's estimate: ~7 million species. Rivet-popper hypothesis (Paul\nEhrlich).</li>\n<li><strong>Evil quartet:</strong> habitat loss and fragmentation (the\nmost important), over-exploitation, alien species invasions (Nile perch,\n<em>Lantana</em>, water hyacinth, <em>Parthenium</em>), co-extinctions\n(25.164). Recent extinctions: dodo, quagga, thylacine, Steller's sea\ncow, three tiger subspecies.</li>\n<li>Conservation: in situ — 34 hotspots worldwide, 3 in India (Western\nGhats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma, Himalaya), biosphere reserves, national\nparks, sanctuaries, sacred groves; ex situ — zoos, botanical gardens,\nseed banks, cryopreservation (24.108, 25.99, 26.124).</li>\n<li>Ramdeo Misra: father of Indian ecology (25.133).</li>\n<li><strong>[T]</strong> Ecology numbers (hotspots, percentages, species\ncounts) are free marks; write them on one flash card.</li>\n</ul>\n"};window.CARDS={"P1": [["Dimensions", "G [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]; h [ML²T⁻¹]; ε₀ [M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A²]; μ₀ [MLT⁻²A⁻²]; c² = 1/(μ₀ε₀); pressure = energy density [ML⁻¹T⁻²]; work = torque = energy [ML²T⁻²]; angle and solid angle are dimensionless; strain, refractive index, relative density have no unit."], ["Significant figures", "multiplication/division keep the fewest sig figs; addition/subtraction keep the fewest decimal places. Zeros between digits and trailing zeros after a decimal point count."], ["[T]", "To test a formula, check that both sides have the same dimensions; a dimension check cannot fix numerical constants (½, 2π)."]], "P2": [["Average speed over equal distances", "2v₁v₂/(v₁ + v₂); over equal times: (v₁ + v₂)/2."], ["Projectile", "T = 2u sinθ/g; H = u²sin²θ/2g; R = u²sin2θ/g; R_max at 45°; H = R tanθ/4; complementary angles (θ, 90° − θ) give the same range."], ["Relative velocity", "v_AB = v_A − v_B (vectors); river-crossing shortest time heads straight across; shortest path needs sinθ = v_river/v_boat."], ["Uniform circular motion", "a = v²/r = ω²r (toward the centre); speed constant, velocity changing."], ["Ruler drop (26.30)", "d = ½gt², so t = √(2d/g)."], ["Graph rules", "slope of x–t = velocity; slope of v–t = acceleration; area under v–t = displacement; area under a–t = change in velocity."], ["[T]", "If a v–t graph is a straight line, v–t slope constant → a–t is a horizontal line."]], "P3": [["Friction", "f_s ≤ μ_sN; f_k = μ_kN; angle of repose tanθ = μ_s; on an incline a = g(sinθ − μcosθ)."], ["Atwood", "a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂); T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂)."], ["Blocks pushed together by force F on m₁", "a = F/(m₁ + m₂); contact force on m₂ = m₂F/(m₁ + m₂)."], ["Lift", "apparent weight = m(g ± a) (up-accelerating +)."], ["Banking", "no friction tanθ = v²/(rg); with friction v_max² = rg(μ + tanθ)/(1 − μtanθ). On a level road v_max = √(μrg)."], ["[T]", "Draw a free-body diagram first; write ΣF along motion = ma for each body, then solve."]], "P4": [["Elastic collision (1D)", "v₁′ = [(m₁ − m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂]/(m₁ + m₂); equal masses exchange velocities; heavy hits light at rest → light moves at ≈2u."], ["Perfectly inelastic", "v = (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂); KE lost = ½[m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂)](u₁ − u₂)²."], ["Vertical circle (string)", "v_top,min = √(gr); v_bottom,min = √(5gr); T_bottom = mg + mv²/r; tension at top is zero when v = √(gr); string goes slack if v_top < √(gr) (25.7)."], ["[T]", "Energy questions with a pendulum: convert KE at the bottom to height with h = v²/2g."]], "P5": [["Moments of inertia (axis through centre)", "ring MR²; disc ½MR²; solid sphere ⅖MR²; hollow sphere ⅔MR²; rod (through centre) ML²/12; rod about end ML²/3; solid cylinder ½MR²."], ["Kinematics", "ω = ω₀ + αt; θ = ω₀t + ½αt²; ω² = ω₀² + 2αθ (revolutions = θ/2π)."], ["Rolling without slipping", "v_cm = ωR; top point 2v, bottom point 0 (24.1); KE_total = ½mv²(1 + k²/R²)."], ["Equilibrium", "ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0 (take torques about a point where an unknown force acts)."], ["Centre of mass", "x_cm = Σmx/Σm; COM does not move without external force."], ["[T]", "Cut sphere/disc: MI is proportional to mass, so find the fraction of mass first."]], "P6": [["Satellite", "KE = GMm/2r; PE = −GMm/r; total E = −GMm/2r; binding energy = +GMm/2r; T² ∝ r³ (Kepler); T = 2π√(r³/GM)."], ["Potential energy at distance r", "U = −GMm/r; work to lift from R to R + h: W = GMmh/[R(R + h)]; for h = R this equals mgR/2 (26.27 type)."], ["Kepler", "law of orbits (ellipse), areas (L conserved), periods (T² ∝ a³)."], ["[T]", "For \"which planet has larger escape speed\", compute √(M/R) ratios."]], "P7": [["Viscosity", "F = 6πηrv (Stokes); terminal velocity v_t = 2r²(ρ − σ)g/9η; viscosity falls with temperature for liquids and rises for gases."], ["Heat", "Q = mcΔT; Q = mL; calorimetry (heat lost = heat gained)."], ["Conduction", "Q/t = kAΔT/L; series equivalent thermal resistance R = L/kA adds; interface temperature of two rods: k₁(T₁ − T)/L₁ = k₂(T − T₂)/L₂ (equal areas)."], ["Radiation", "Stefan P = σAT⁴; Wien λ_m T = b; Newton's cooling rate ∝ temperature difference."], ["[T]", "Wire-stretch problems: if volume is fixed and length is multiplied by n, resistance (and A change) → area ÷ n."]], "P8": [["First law", "Q = ΔU + W; ΔU = nC_vΔT (any process, ideal gas)."], ["Adiabatic", "PVᵞ = const; TVᵞ⁻¹ = const; W = (P₁V₁ − P₂V₂)/(γ − 1) = nR(T₁ − T₂)/(γ − 1)."], ["Isobaric heat split for an ideal gas", "ΔU/Q = 1/γ, W/Q = 1 − 1/γ (monatomic: 60% to ΔU, 40% to work; diatomic: 71%, 29%)."], ["Cyclic process", "ΔU = 0; net work = area enclosed; Q_net = W_net."], ["Carnot", "η = 1 − T₂/T₁ (kelvin); refrigerator COP = T₂/(T₁ − T₂)."], ["Second law", "no 100% efficient engine; heat flows hot to cold spontaneously."], ["[T]", "For a P–V loop, clockwise = work done by gas (positive)."]], "P9": [["Degrees of freedom f", "U = (f/2)nRT; C_v = fR/2; γ = 1 + 2/f. Monatomic f = 3; diatomic 5 (7 if vibration counted → γ = 9/7, C_v = 7R/2); law of equipartition: ½kT per DOF."], ["[T]", "Same T, different gases: KE equal, but rms speed depends on mass; the heavier one is slower."]], "P10": [["Spring", "T = 2π√(m/k); series k_eq = k₁k₂/(k₁ + k₂); parallel k₁ + k₂; cutting a spring to 1/n length multiplies k by n."], ["Energy", "total E = ½kA² (constant); KE and PE oscillate at twice the SHM frequency."], ["Floating cylinder/cork", "T = 2π√(m/(Aρg)) = 2π√(h/g) where h is the submerged depth."], ["Waves", "v = fλ; v = √(T/μ) on a string; y = A sin(kx − ωt); phase difference = 2πΔx/λ = ωΔt."], ["Standing waves", "string/open pipe fₙ = nv/2L (n = 1, 2, 3); closed pipe fₙ = (2n − 1)v/4L (odd harmonics only); end correction e ≈ 0.6r."], ["Resonance-tube", "v = 2f(L₂ − L₁); beats f_b = |f₁ − f₂|; Doppler f′ = f(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s) (approach raises pitch)."], ["[T]", "In a pipe problem, the number of nodes/antinodes follows from the harmonic: closed pipe has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end."]], "P11": [["Gauss", "∮E·dA = q/ε₀. Infinite line E = λ/2πε₀r; infinite sheet E = σ/2ε₀; conductor surface E = σ/ε₀; spherical shell: E = 0 inside, V = constant inside and equal to the surface value, outside behaves like a point charge."], ["Conductor", "E = 0 inside, charge on the outer surface, surface equipotential; field inside a cavity (no charge in cavity) = 0."], ["Capacitor", "C = ε₀A/d; with dielectric C = Kε₀A/d; with partial slab C = ε₀A/(d − t + t/K)."], ["Battery connected", "V constant. Battery disconnected: Q constant. Plates brought closer: C increases; if disconnected, V falls, U falls (24.46)."], ["Two capacitors joined", "common V = (C₁V₁ + C₂V₂)/(C₁ + C₂); energy loss = ½·[C₁C₂/(C₁ + C₂)](V₁ − V₂)²."], ["[T]", "Capacitor network: first find which capacitors are in series; unequal-branch bridge networks need symmetry (balance point has equal potential)."]], "P12": [["Cell", "V = E − Ir (discharging); series EMFs add; for n identical cells in parallel, E stays, r/n."], ["Kirchhoff", "ΣI = 0 at a node; ΣV = 0 around a loop."], ["Heaters", "P_series = P₁P₂/(P₁ + P₂); P_parallel = P₁ + P₂."], ["Ideal diode in a circuit", "forward-biased = a wire; reverse-biased = an open switch (24.44)."], ["[T]", "Symmetrical networks: points at equal potential can be joined or removed; no current flows between them."]], "P13": [["Biot–Savart", "loop centre B = μ₀NI/2R; on axis B = μ₀NIR²/[2(R² + x²)^{3/2}]; arc of angle θ: B = μ₀Iθ/4πR; long straight wire B = μ₀I/2πr; solenoid B = μ₀nI; inside a straight wire of radius a (uniform current): B = μ₀Ir/2πa² (zero at the axis, maximum at the surface)."], ["Velocity selector (E ⟂ B)", "v = E/B passes undeflected."], ["Loop", "moment M = NIA; torque τ = MB sinθ; U = −MB cosθ; magnet bar-field axis B = μ₀·2M/4πr³."], ["Moving-coil galvanometer", "shunt S = I_gG/(I − I_g) for an ammeter; series resistance R = V/I_g − G for a voltmeter; current sensitivity θ/I = NAB/C."], ["Materials", "diamagnetic χ small negative; paramagnetic χ small positive ∝ 1/T (Curie law); ferromagnetic χ large positive, above Curie temperature becomes paramagnetic; μ_r = 1 + χ."], ["A bar magnet bent at its midpoint into an L shape", "moment becomes M/√2; cut across the length: M halves; cut along the length: M halves."], ["[T]", "Right-hand rule for direction; for a coil, N turns multiplies the field at the centre by N."]], "P14": [["LR growth", "I = I₀(1 − e^{−t/τ}), τ = L/R."], ["Lenz", "induced current opposes the change; eddy currents damp motion (induction furnace, magnetic braking)."], ["Resonance", "ω₀ = 1/√(LC); at resonance Z = R (minimum), I maximum, φ = 0; quality factor Q = ω₀L/R."], ["Capacitor in AC", "I₀ = V₀ωC, current leads voltage by 90°; inductor: current lags by 90°."], ["Transformer (ideal)", "V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p = I_p/I_s; step-up has more turns on the secondary."], ["[T]", "For \"peak value reached at time t\", solve sin(ωt) = 1 → t = T/4."]], "P15": [["Spectrum in increasing frequency", "radio < microwave < infrared < visible < ultraviolet < X-ray < gamma. Uses: microwave (radar, oven), infrared (remote, greenhouse effect), UV (sterilisation, LASIK), X-ray (imaging), gamma (cancer therapy)."], ["Electric and magnetic energy densities are equal", "u = ½ε₀E² = B²/2μ₀; intensity I = ½ε₀cE₀²; momentum p = U/c."], ["[T]", "Wave equation check: E and B oscillate in phase and perpendicular; direction of travel = E × B."]], "P16": [["Prism", "n = sin[(A + δ_m)/2]/sin(A/2); small angle δ = (n − 1)A."], ["Microscope", "M ≈ (L/f_o)(D/f_e) at final image at infinity; telescope M = f_o/f_e, tube length f_o + f_e."], ["Young's", "fringe width β = λD/d; path difference = d sinθ = yd/D; maxima at nλ, minima at (n + ½)λ; Iₘₐₓ/Iₘᵢₙ = (√I₁ + √I₂)²/(√I₁ − √I₂)²; with white light the central fringe is white and the first colour fringe has violet nearest the centre."], ["Single slit", "minima at a sinθ = nλ; central maximum width 2λD/a (twice the others)."], ["[T]", "For lens combinations, find the first image and treat it as the object for the second lens."]], "P17": [["Photoelectric", "KE_max = hν − φ; eV₀ = KE_max; threshold ν₀ = φ/h. Emission is instantaneous, depends on frequency not intensity."], ["[T]", "For a \"match list\" question, link each pair: E = hν ↔ particle nature, λ = h/p ↔ de Broglie, diffraction ↔ wave nature, Compton effect ↔ photon momentum."]], "P18": [["Bohr", "r_n = 0.529n²/Z Å; v_n = 2.19 × 10⁶ Z/n m/s; E_n = −13.6Z²/n² eV; ΔE = hc/λ."], ["Series", "Lyman → n = 1 (UV); Balmer → n = 2 (visible); Paschen → n = 3 (IR). Balmer lines: 3→2 656.3 nm, 4→2 486.1 nm, 5→2 434.1 nm, 6→2 410.2 nm (24.2)."], ["Nucleus", "R = R₀A^{1/3}, R₀ ≈ 1.2 fm; nuclear density is constant (≈ 2.3 × 10¹⁷ kg/m³), independent of A; volume ∝ A."], ["Decay", "N = N₀e^{−λt}; T½ = 0.693/λ; mean life τ = 1/λ; activity A = λN; after n half-lives N = N₀/2ⁿ."], ["Rutherford", "distance of closest approach r₀ = 2Ze²/(4πε₀K); most α particles go undeviated; very few (1 in 8000) deflected through large angles."], ["[T]", "For decay chains, count α (4 mass units each) from ΔA, then fix Z with β."]], "P19": [["Semiconductor gaps", "Ge 0.67 eV, Si 1.1 eV; conductors overlap, insulators > 3 eV."], ["n-type", "pentavalent donor (P, As, Sb); p-type: trivalent acceptor (B, Al, Ga, In); nᵢ² = np; the whole crystal stays neutral."], ["Diode", "forward bias reduces depletion width, conducts above ~0.7 V (Si), ~0.3 V (Ge); reverse bias widens the depletion layer."], ["Zener", "used in reverse breakdown as a voltage regulator. LED: forward-biased, emits light. Photodiode: reverse-biased, current ∝ light. Solar cell: no external bias; works in the fourth quadrant."], ["Logic gates", "OR A + B; AND A·B; NOT A′; NAND (A·B)′; NOR (A + B)′. NAND and NOR are universal gates."], ["De Morgan", "(A + B)′ = A′B′; (AB)′ = A′ + B′."], ["[T]", "For a gate circuit, write the Boolean output step by step and then fill the truth table."]], "P20": [["Half-deflection method", "G = RS/(R − S); figure of merit k = E/[(R + G)θ]."], ["Diode characteristics", "forward and reverse curves; Zener reverse breakdown voltage."], ["[T]", "If the question gives a zero error, correct the reading first, then apply other steps."]], "C1": [["Limiting reagent", "divide moles by the stoichiometric coefficient; the smallest ratio is limiting."], ["Empirical formula", "% → moles → divide by smallest → whole ratio; molecular formula = n × empirical (n = molar mass/empirical mass)."], ["Count atoms", "urea CO(NH₂)₂ = CH₄N₂O has 4 H per molecule."], ["Laws", "conservation of mass, definite proportions, multiple proportions, Gay-Lussac's gaseous volumes, Avogadro."], ["Dalton's limits", "cannot explain isotopes, allotropes, sub-atomic particles; the atom is divisible."], ["[T]", "Always convert everything to moles first; ratios of moles give answers."]], "C2": [["Bohr", "Eₙ = −13.6Z²/n² eV (−2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ Z²/n² J); rₙ = 52.9n²/Z pm; vₙ ∝ Z/n."], ["Quantum numbers", "n ≥ 1; l = 0 … n − 1; m = −l … +l; s = ±½. Orbitals in a shell n²; electrons 2n²; orbitals in a subshell 2l + 1."], ["Nodes", "radial nodes = n − l − 1; angular nodes = l; total = n − 1."], ["[T]", "Nodes: write \"n − l − 1\" for radial, \"l\" for angular; plug in."]], "C3": [["VSEPR", "2 pairs linear; 3 trigonal planar; 4 tetrahedral (CH₄), NH₃ pyramidal (107°), H₂O bent (104.5°); 5 trigonal bipyramidal (PCl₅), SF₄ see-saw, ClF₃ T-shaped, XeF₂ linear; 6 octahedral (SF₆), BrF₅ square pyramidal, XeF₄ square planar; 7 pentagonal bipyramidal (IF₇)."], ["Bond orders and magnetism", "N₂ 3 (dia); N₂⁺ 2.5 (para); O₂⁺ 2.5 (para); O₂ 2 (para); O₂⁻ 1.5 (para); O₂²⁻ 1 (dia); C₂ 2 (dia); B₂ 1 (para); Ne₂ 0 (does not exist). Stability follows bond order: O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂⁻ > O₂²⁻."], ["Fajans", "small, highly charged cation + large anion → more covalent. Formal charge = V − N − B/2."], ["Hydrogen bonding", "intramolecular (o-nitrophenol, chelation; more volatile, lower b.p.) vs intermolecular (p-isomer; higher b.p.). Hydride b.p.: H₂O > HF > NH₃ (anomalous); then rises down the group for others."], ["Bond length", "triple < double < single; bond energy reverse."], ["Bond angle", "CH₄ 109.5°, NH₃ 107°, H₂O 104.5°, BeCl₂ 180°, BF₃ 120°."], ["[T]", "For MOT questions, count total electrons first; add one electron to π* (O₂⁻), remove from π* (O₂⁺)."]], "C4": [["Spontaneity", "ΔH − and ΔS + → always; ΔH + and ΔS − → never; ΔH − and ΔS − → low T; ΔH + and ΔS + → high T."], ["Free expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum", "w = 0, q = 0, ΔU = 0, but ΔS > 0 (R.59)."], ["State functions", "U, H, S, G, T, P, V; not q or w. Extensive: U, H, S, G, V; intensive: T, P, density."], ["[T]", "If Δn_g = 0, ΔH = ΔU."]], "C5": [["van't Hoff factor", "dissociation i = 1 + α(n − 1); association i = 1 − α(1 − 1/n); i > 1 dissociation, i < 1 association."], ["Henry", "p = K_H x (higher K_H, lower solubility); solubility of gases falls with temperature."], ["[T]", "For boiling-point elevation, count particles first (i), then compare."]], "C6": [["Le Chatelier", "heating favours the endothermic direction; adding an inert gas at constant volume has no effect, at constant pressure shifts toward more moles; catalyst does not shift equilibrium."], ["Buffers", "pH = pKₐ + log([salt]/[acid]); pOH = pK_b + log([salt]/[base]); maximum buffer capacity at pH = pKₐ."], ["Salt pH", "WA + SB: pH = 7 + ½(pKₐ + log C); SA + WB: pH = 7 − ½(pK_b + log C); WA + WB: pH = 7 + ½(pKₐ − pK_b)."], ["Solubility from K_sp", "AB s = √K_sp; AB₂ s = (K_sp/4)^{1/3}; A₂B₃ s = (K_sp/108)^{1/5}; common ion lowers solubility; precipitation when Q > K_sp."], ["Polyprotic", "K_a1 ≫ K_a2; [H⁺] comes almost entirely from the first step."], ["Physical equilibria", "Henry's law, vapour pressure; Lewis acid (electron pair acceptor), Brønsted (proton donor)."], ["[T]", "Degree of dissociation: set up ICE table; for small α, 1 − α ≈ 1."]], "C7": [["Oxidation states", "O −2 (peroxide −1, superoxide −½, OF₂ +2); H +1 (−1 in metal hydrides); sum = charge."], ["Balancing", "ion–electron method (acidic: add H⁺/H₂O; basic: add OH⁻)."], ["Faraday", "W = ZIt = (E/96500)It; 1 F = 96500 C = 1 mol e⁻. Cu²⁺ needs 2 F per mol; Al³⁺ 3 F per mol; Ag⁺ 1 F."], ["Conductance", "κ = G(ℓ/A); Λₘ = κ × 1000/C; strong electrolytes Λₘ = Λ°ₘ − A√C; weak electrolytes α = Λₘ/Λ°ₘ; Kohlrausch: Λ°ₘ = ν₊λ°₊ + ν₋λ°₋."], ["Electrolysis products", "aqueous CuSO₄ (inert electrodes) → Cu at cathode, O₂ at anode; dilute H₂SO₄ → H₂ and O₂; aqueous NaCl → H₂ at cathode, Cl₂ at anode."], ["Batteries", "dry cell (Zn, MnO₂), lead accumulator (discharge: Pb, PbO₂, H₂SO₄ → PbSO₄), fuel cell H₂–O₂ (efficiency ~70%)."], ["[T]", "A \"non-redox\" reaction (24.53) is one where every oxidation number is unchanged; check each element."]], "C8": [["Zero order", "[A] = [A]₀ − kt; t½ = [A]₀/2k; completes in [A]₀/k."], ["First order", "k = (2.303/t) log([A]₀/[A]); t½ = 0.693/k (independent of concentration); t₇₅ = 2t½; t₉₉.₉ = 10t½; N = N₀/2ⁿ with n = t/t½."], ["Plots", "zero order [A] vs t linear; first order ln[A] vs t linear."], ["Rule of thumb", "rate roughly doubles per 10 °C."], ["[T]", "If t½ is fixed, you have a first-order reaction."]], "C9": [["Period 2 IE order", "Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne."], ["Electron gain enthalpy (most negative)", "Cl > F > Br > I; noble gases positive; S more negative than O."], ["Electronegativity", "F 4.0 > O 3.5 > N = Cl 3.0 > Br 2.8."], ["Isoelectronic ions", "higher nuclear charge → smaller (N³⁻ > O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Al³⁺)."], ["Oxides", "basic (left) → amphoteric (Al₂O₃, ZnO, BeO) → acidic (right)."], ["Nomenclature digits (IUPAC for Z > 100)", "0 nil, 1 un, 2 bi, 3 tri, 4 quad, 5 pent, 6 hex, 7 sept, 8 oct, 9 enn."], ["[T]", "Write the period-2 IE order from memory and compare any question with it."]], "C10": [["Group 13", "B is a non-metal, electron deficient (BF₃ is a Lewis acid); boric acid B(OH)₃ + H₂O → [B(OH)₄]⁻ + H⁺ (Lewis acid, monobasic); diborane B₂H₆ has two 3c–2e bridge bonds; maximum covalency of B is 4 (no d orbitals); inert-pair effect: Tl⁺ more stable than Tl³⁺; Al is amphoteric."], ["Group 14", "catenation C ≫ Si; allotropes diamond (sp³), graphite (sp², conductor), fullerene C₆₀ (sp²)."], ["Group 15", "N₂ inert; hydride basic strength NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃ > BiH₃; bond angle falls down the group; NH₃ has the anomalously high boiling point; oxoacids H₃PO₂ (monobasic, reducing), H₃PO₃ (dibasic), H₃PO₄ (tribasic)."], ["Group 16", "ozone O₃ oxidises PbS → PbSO₄; SO₂ is a reducing, bleaching agent; H₂SO₄ by the contact process (V₂O₅ catalyst); oxidation states +2, +4, +6; acidic strength H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te."], ["Group 17", "F₂ is the strongest oxidiser; acid strength HF < HCl < HBr < HI; oxoacid strength HClO < HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO₄; Cl₂ + cold dilute NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl; Cl₂ + hot conc. NaOH → NaCl + NaClO₃; NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ → HCl, while NaBr/NaI give Br₂/I₂; interhalogens ClF₃ (T-shaped), IF₇ (pentagonal bipyramidal)."], ["Group 18", "XeF₂ linear (sp³d), XeF₄ square planar (sp³d²), XeF₆ distorted octahedral (sp³d³), XeO₃ pyramidal (sp³), XeOF₄ square pyramidal; He has the lowest boiling point; first noble gas compound XePtF₆."], ["Potash alum K₂SO₄·Al₂(SO₄)₃·24H₂O", "K⁺ : Al³⁺ : SO₄²⁻ = 1 : 1 : 2 (R.68)."], ["[T]", "Hybridisation of Xe fluorides = (8 + number of F)/2 electron pairs → sp³d, sp³d², sp³d³."]], "C11": [["Melting points in 3d", "rise to V–Cr, dip at Mn (half-filled stability, weak metallic bonding), lowest for Zn (R.84)."], ["Catalysts", "Fe (Haber), V₂O₅ (contact), Ni (hydrogenation), TiCl₄ + Al(C₂H₅)₃ (Ziegler–Natta), PdCl₂ (Wacker), MnO₂ (KClO₃ decomposition)."], ["KMnO₄", "2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂ (heating at 513 K) (R.50). As oxidant: acidic MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O (n = 5); neutral/weakly alkaline → MnO₂ (n = 3); strongly alkaline → MnO₄²⁻ (n = 1)."], ["K₂Cr₂O₇", "Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O; chromate (yellow, CrO₄²⁻) ⇌ dichromate (orange, Cr₂O₇²⁻) by pH."], ["Lanthanoid contraction", "Zr and Hf have similar radii; basicity of Ln(OH)₃ falls from La to Lu."], ["Ln³⁺ f-electron counts", "La f⁰, Nd f³, Gd f⁷, Lu f¹⁴; diamagnetic: La³⁺, Lu³⁺, Ce⁴⁺ (f⁰); Ce⁴⁺ is a strong oxidant; Eu²⁺ (f⁷), Yb²⁺ (f¹⁴). Actinoids: radioactive, oxidation states up to +7."], ["[T]", "For paramagnetism questions, write the d- or f-configuration, count unpaired electrons, then use √(n(n + 2))."]], "C12": [["Ligands", "monodentate NH₃, H₂O, CN⁻, Cl⁻; bidentate en, oxalate; hexadentate EDTA; ambidentate NO₂⁻ (N or O), SCN⁻ (S or N), CN⁻ (C or N)."], ["Naming", "ligands alphabetically, anionic ligands end in -o (chlorido, cyanido), cation complex then metal oxidation state in Roman numerals; anionic complex: metal name ends in -ate (ferrate, cuprate, argentate, aurate, stannate)."], ["Isomers", "geometrical (square planar MA₂B₂; octahedral MA₄B₂ cis/trans, MA₃B₃ fac/mer); optical (octahedral [M(AA)₃], cis-[M(AA)₂B₂]); ionisation, hydrate, linkage, coordination (e.g. [Co(NH₃)₆][Cr(CN)₆]). Tetrahedral complexes show no geometrical isomerism."], ["Hybridisation/magnetism", "strong-field ligands (CN⁻, CO, en) → low-spin, inner-orbital d²sp³ (octahedral); weak-field (H₂O, F⁻, halides) → high-spin, outer-orbital sp³d². [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ diamagnetic, [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ 1 unpaired e⁻; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ dsp² square planar, diamagnetic; [NiCl₄]²⁻ sp³ tetrahedral, paramagnetic; Ni(CO)₄ sp³, diamagnetic."], ["Spectrochemical series", "I⁻ < Br⁻ < S²⁻ < SCN⁻ < Cl⁻ < NO₃⁻ < F⁻ < OH⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < H₂O < NCS⁻ < EDTA⁴⁻ < NH₃ < en < bpy < phen < NO₂⁻ < CN⁻ < CO."], ["Colour", "a complex absorbs the complementary colour; larger Δ → absorbs shorter wavelength (λ ∝ 1/Δ)."], ["Conductivity (ions)", "[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ 4; [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ 3; [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl 2; [Co(NH₃)₃Cl₃] 0. Free (ionisable) Cl⁻ = moles of AgCl precipitated."], ["Metal carbonyls", "synergic bonding (σ donation + π back-donation) strengthens M–C and weakens C≡O."], ["Importance", "chelate effect, qualitative analysis, metal extraction, haemoglobin (Fe), chlorophyll (Mg), vitamin B₁₂ (Co)."], ["[T]", "Oxidation state first, then d-count, then ligand strength, then spin and magnetism."]], "C13": [["Methods", "crystallisation, sublimation (naphthalene, camphor), simple distillation (large b.p. gap), fractional distillation, vacuum distillation (glycerol), steam distillation (aniline, nitrobenzene), differential extraction, chromatography (R_f = solute distance/solvent distance)."], ["Lassaigne", "Na fusion makes NaCN, Na₂S, NaX. N: FeSO₄ → Prussian blue; S: sodium nitroprusside → violet (or lead acetate → black PbS); N and S together → blood-red Fe(SCN)²⁺; halogens: AgNO₃ after boiling with HNO₃ (white AgCl, pale-yellow AgBr, yellow AgI); P: oxidise to phosphate, ammonium molybdate → yellow ppt."], ["Dumas (N as N₂)", "%N = (28 × V × 100)/(22400 × w); Kjeldahl: %N = 1.4 × N × V/w (not for nitro, azo, ring N)."], ["Carius", "%Cl = (35.5/143.5)(m_AgCl/m) × 100; %S = (32/233)(m_BaSO₄/m) × 100; %C = (12/44)(m_CO₂/m) × 100; %H = (2/18)(m_H₂O/m) × 100."], ["[T]", "Learn the formulas as fractions: atomic mass/molar mass of the weighed product."]], "C14": [["Priority of groups", "COOH > SO₃H > COOR > COX > CONH₂ > CN > CHO > C=O > OH > NH₂ > C=C > C≡C. Longest chain with the principal group, lowest locants, alphabetical order."], ["Carbocation stability", "3° > 2° > 1° > CH₃⁺; benzyl and allyl are resonance-stabilised."], ["Inductive effect", "−NO₂ > −CN > −F > −Cl > −Br > −I; +I: tert-alkyl > iso-propyl > ethyl > methyl. +M: −OH, −OR, −NH₂, halogens (weak); −M: −NO₂, −CN, −CHO, −COOH."], ["Isomerism", "chain, position, functional, metamerism (same functional group, different alkyl groups on either side, e.g. ethers), tautomerism; geometrical needs two different groups on each C of the double bond; optical needs a chiral centre (2ⁿ stereoisomers)."], ["Intermediates", "carbocation sp² planar; carbanion sp³ pyramidal; radical sp² planar."], ["[T]", "For a name question, find the principal chain first, then number to give the principal group the smallest locant."]], "C15": [["Alkanes", "more branching → lower boiling point; free-radical halogenation (initiation, propagation, termination); selectivity 3° > 2° > 1° (Cl: ~5 : 3.8 : 1; Br is far more selective). Staggered ethane is the most stable conformer. Number of monochlorination products = number of distinct hydrogen types."], ["Alkenes", "HX adds by Markovnikov; peroxide effect only for HBr (anti-Markovnikov); hydration Markovnikov; hydroboration–oxidation anti-Markovnikov, syn addition. Ozonolysis (O₃, Zn/H₂O) gives aldehydes/ketones. Cold dilute alkaline KMnO₄ gives cis-diol; hot acidic gives acids/ketones/CO₂. Br₂/CCl₄ or Baeyer reagent tests unsaturation."], ["Alkynes", "terminal alkynes are acidic (react with Na, NaNH₂, ammoniacal AgNO₃ → white ppt, Cu₂Cl₂ → red ppt); Lindlar's catalyst → cis-alkene; Na/liquid NH₃ → trans-alkene; hydration (HgSO₄/H₂SO₄) gives ketone (acetaldehyde from ethyne); acidity from s-character sp > sp² > sp³; C–C bond length ethane > ethene > ethyne."], ["Benzene", "Hückel (4n + 2)π electrons, planar, cyclic, conjugated. Preparations: decarboxylation of sodium benzoate, trimerisation of ethyne (hot iron tube), reduction of phenol with zinc dust. EAS: nitration (NO₂⁺), halogenation (X₂ + FeX₃), sulphonation, Friedel–Crafts alkylation and acylation (fails on strongly deactivated rings). Benzene + Cl₂ with FeCl₃ → chlorobenzene (substitution); with Cl₂ in sunlight → hexachlorocyclohexane (addition) (26.51)."], ["Directing", "o/p (activating) −OH, −NH₂, −OR, −R; o/p (deactivating) halogens; meta: −NO₂, −CN, −CHO, −COR, −COOH, −SO₃H."], ["[T]", "Count monochlorination products by listing H types after drawing symmetry."]], "C16": [["S_N1", "3° > 2° > 1°, carbocation, racemisation, polar protic solvent. S_N2: 1° > 2° > 3°, inversion (Walden), polar aprotic solvent. Leaving group I > Br > Cl > F."], ["Preparation", "alcohol + SOCl₂ (best, gaseous by-products), PCl₅, PCl₃, HX/ZnCl₂; Finkelstein (NaI/acetone → R–I); Swarts (AgF → R–F); Sandmeyer (CuCl/HCl, CuBr/HBr); Gattermann (Cu powder)."], ["[T]", "Alkyl halide + AgNO₃ (aq): the order of precipitate speed follows carbocation stability."]], "C17": [["Alcohol preparation", "hydroboration, hydration, Grignard + HCHO (1°), + other aldehydes (2°), + ketones (3°); LiAlH₄ reduces aldehydes, ketones, acids, esters; NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes and ketones only."], ["Lucas (ZnCl₂/conc. HCl)", "3° immediate turbidity; 2° in ~5 min; 1° none at room temperature."], ["Oxidation", "PCC stops at aldehyde (1°); K₂Cr₂O₇/KMnO₄ gives acid; 2° → ketone; 3° resists. Dehydration: conc. H₂SO₄ at 443 K → alkene; 413 K → ether (1°); ease 3° > 2° > 1°. Acidity: 1° > 2° > 3°; phenol is more acidic than alcohol."], ["Phenol acidity rises with EWG", "picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol) > p-nitrophenol > m-nitrophenol > phenol > cresols. Reactions: Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH → salicylaldehyde), Kolbe (CO₂/NaOH → salicylic acid), Br₂ water → 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt), neutral FeCl₃ → violet."], ["Williamson ether", "RONa + R′X (S_N2, use a primary halide)."], ["Aldehydes/ketones, preparation", "Rosenmund (RCOCl, H₂/Pd–BaSO₄), Etard (toluene + CrO₂Cl₂ → benzaldehyde, R.46), Stephen (RCN + SnCl₂/HCl), DIBAL-H (esters/nitriles at low temperature, 25.83), Gattermann–Koch (benzene + CO/HCl/AlCl₃), Friedel–Crafts acylation, Grignard + nitrile → ketone (25.78), Cu/573 K dehydrogenation of 1° alcohols."], ["Nucleophilic addition", "HCN (cyanohydrin), NaHSO₃, NH₂OH (oxime), NH₂NH₂ (hydrazone), 2,4-DNP (orange-red ppt; test for C=O). Reactivity HCHO > RCHO > ArCHO > ketones."], ["Aldol needs α-H (dil. NaOH); Cannizzaro (no α-H", "HCHO, benzaldehyde; conc. alkali) → alcohol + acid salt; haloform (NaOH + I₂) for CH₃CO–R and CH₃CH(OH)R → yellow iodoform (acetophenone gives it, R.90)."], ["Reduction", "Clemmensen (Zn–Hg/HCl; not for acid-sensitive), Wolff–Kishner (NH₂NH₂/KOH; not for base-sensitive)."], ["Tests", "Tollens (silver mirror) – all aldehydes; Fehling (red Cu₂O) – aliphatic aldehydes only, not aromatic aldehydes or ketones (24.66); formic acid reduces both."], ["Carboxylic acid acidity", "HCOOH > CH₃COOH; Cl₃CCOOH > Cl₂CHCOOH > ClCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH; benzoic acid (pKₐ 4.2) stronger than acetic (4.76); EDG weakens, EWG strengthens (p-nitrobenzoic strongest). Reactions: HVZ α-halogenation, esterification, NaHCO₃ effervescence (distinguishes acids from phenols), SOCl₂ → acyl chloride, LiAlH₄ → alcohol, soda-lime decarboxylation."], ["Boiling points (similar M)", "acids > alcohols > aldehydes/ketones > ethers > alkanes."], ["[T]", "Name reactions: list each with its reagent in one line and revise daily."]], "C18": [["Basic strength in water", "(C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃; methyl series (CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₃N > NH₃; aryl amines are weaker (aniline < NH₃; p-toluidine > aniline > p-nitroaniline). Gas phase: 3° > 2° > 1°."], ["Preparation", "Gabriel phthalimide (1° aliphatic only); Hoffmann bromamide (RCONH₂ + Br₂/NaOH → RNH₂, one C fewer); nitrile reduction (RCN → RCH₂NH₂); nitro reduction (Sn/HCl, Fe/HCl, H₂/Pd); ammonolysis."], ["Hinsberg (C₆H₅SO₂Cl)", "1° → sulphonamide soluble in alkali; 2° → insoluble; 3° → no reaction. Carbylamine (CHCl₃ + KOH): only 1° amines, foul-smelling isocyanide (26.89)."], ["Nitrous acid", "aliphatic 1° → alcohol + N₂; aromatic 1° at 273–278 K → diazonium salt; 2° → N-nitrosoamine (yellow oil)."], ["Diazonium", "Sandmeyer (CuCl, CuBr, CuCN), Gattermann (Cu powder), I via KI (no Cu), F via HBF₄, H via H₃PO₂, OH via warm water; coupling with phenol (pH 9–10) and aniline (pH 4–5) → azo dyes at the para position. Aniline + NaNO₂ + HCl at 0–5 °C → benzenediazonium chloride (25.74)."], ["[T]", "For \"amine order\" questions, check solvent: water (2° > 3°) or gas phase (3° > 2°)."]], "C19": [["Glucose", "aldohexose; HI → n-hexane (linear chain); Br₂ water → gluconic acid; HNO₃ → saccharic acid; α-D-(+)-glucose and β-form are anomers (differ at C-1); mutarotation. Fructose is a ketohexose."], ["Starch", "amylose (α-1,4, ~20%) + amylopectin (α-1,4 and α-1,6, ~80%); cellulose is β-1,4; glycogen resembles amylopectin."], ["Proteins", "peptide bond (–CONH–); primary sequence, secondary (α-helix, β-sheet, hydrogen bonds), tertiary folding, quaternary (e.g. haemoglobin α₂β₂). Denaturation (heat, pH) breaks secondary and tertiary structure, not the primary."], ["Vitamins", "A (night blindness, xerophthalmia), B₁ (beri-beri), B₂ (cheilosis), B₆ (convulsions), B₁₂ (pernicious anaemia), C (scurvy), D (rickets, osteomalacia), E (RBC fragility), K (poor clotting), biotin (dermatitis). Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K; water-soluble: B group and C."], ["Nucleic acids", "nucleoside = sugar + base; nucleotide = + phosphate; DNA has deoxyribose and A, G, C, T; RNA has ribose and A, G, C, U; A=T (2 H-bonds), G≡C (3 H-bonds)."], ["Hormones", "insulin (polypeptide), thyroxine (amino-acid derivative), estradiol (steroid)."], ["[T]", "Vitamin–disease pairs: learn as a two-column table and recite."]], "C20": [["Cation groups", "I Pb²⁺ (dil. HCl, white PbCl₂); II Cu²⁺ (H₂S in dil. HCl, black CuS); III Al³⁺ (white gelatinous), Fe³⁺ (reddish brown) (NH₄Cl + NH₄OH); IV Zn²⁺ (white ZnS), Ni²⁺ (black NiS) (H₂S in ammoniacal medium); V Ca²⁺, Ba²⁺ ((NH₄)₂CO₃, white carbonates); VI Mg²⁺ (Na₂HPO₄ → white ppt). NH₄⁺: Nessler's reagent (brown) or NaOH + heat → NH₃."], ["Anions", "CO₃²⁻ (dil. HCl, CO₂ turns lime water milky); S²⁻ (H₂S, lead acetate paper black; nitroprusside violet); SO₃²⁻ (SO₂, turns acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ green); NO₂⁻ (brown fumes, starch-iodide blue); NO₃⁻ (brown ring test with FeSO₄ and conc. H₂SO₄); Cl⁻ (AgNO₃ white curdy ppt, soluble in NH₄OH; chromyl chloride test red vapours); Br⁻ (pale-yellow ppt, partially soluble; CS₂ layer orange-brown); I⁻ (yellow ppt, insoluble; CS₂ layer violet). Acetate: vinegar smell with dil. H₂SO₄, neutral FeCl₃ red colour, fruity ester smell with ethanol + conc. H₂SO₄ (26.60). The OCR of the official list may have dropped acetate; the 2026 paper tested it, so include it."], ["Titrations", "KMnO₄ vs oxalic acid in dilute H₂SO₄ at ~60–70 °C, self-indicator, permanent light pink end point, never HCl or HNO₃; 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O. Mohr's salt FeSO₄·(NH₄)₂SO₄·6H₂O vs KMnO₄ (Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺). Phenolphthalein (8.3–10) for weak acid vs strong base (oxalic acid vs NaOH); methyl orange (3.1–4.4) for strong acid vs weak base."], ["Preparations", "Mohr's salt (equimolar FeSO₄ and (NH₄)₂SO₄ in dilute H₂SO₄, crystallise); potash alum (K₂SO₄ + Al₂(SO₄)₃); acetanilide (aniline + acetic anhydride); p-nitroacetanilide (nitration of acetanilide, para major); aniline yellow (diazonium + aniline); iodoform (ethanol or acetone + I₂/NaOH)."], ["Phthalein test for phenol", "phenol + phthalic anhydride + conc. H₂SO₄ → phenolphthalein, pink in alkali (26.74)."], ["Experiments", "enthalpy of solution of CuSO₄; enthalpy of neutralisation of strong acid and strong base (≈ −57 kJ/mol); lyophilic (starch, gelatin) and lyophobic (Fe(OH)₃, As₂S₃) sols; I⁻ + H₂O₂ kinetic study."], ["[T]", "Group-separation order: \"Pb, Cu, Al-Fe, Zn-Ni, Ca-Ba, Mg\" (I to VI)."]], "B1": [["Hierarchy", "species → genus → family → order → class → phylum/division → kingdom. [T] \"Kids Prefer Cheese Over Fried Green Spinach\" (K P C O F G S reversed)."], ["Binomial nomenclature (Linnaeus)", "genus capitalised, species lower case, both italic or underlined separately; author name after the species."], ["Fungi", "Phycomycetes (Mucor, Rhizopus; coenocytic), Ascomycetes (Penicillium, Aspergillus, Neurospora; conidia and ascospores), Basidiomycetes (Agaricus, Ustilago, Puccinia), Deuteromycetes (Alternaria, Trichoderma). Plasmogamy → dikaryophase → karyogamy (R.96)."], ["Algae", "Chlorophyceae (chl a, b; starch; Chlamydomonas, Volvox, Spirogyra, Chara); Phaeophyceae (chl a, c, fucoxanthin; mannitol, laminarin; Laminaria, Fucus); Rhodophyceae (chl a, d; phycoerythrin; floridean starch; Polysiphonia, Porphyra, Gracilaria and Gelidium for agar; Chondrus for carrageen)."], ["Bryophytes", "gametophyte dominant; gemmae in Marchantia (25.168); moss protonema. Pteridophytes: sporophyte dominant; classes Psilopsida (Psilotum), Lycopsida (Selaginella, Lycopodium), Sphenopsida (Equisetum), Pteropsida (ferns); heterospory in Selaginella and Salvinia. Gymnosperms: naked ovules; Cycas, Pinus, Ginkgo; coralloid roots (Cycas)."], ["Animals", "Porifera (cellular level, choanocytes), Cnidaria (diploblastic, cnidoblasts), Ctenophora (comb plates), Platyhelminthes (acoelomate, flame cells), Aschelminthes (pseudocoelomate), Annelida (metameric, true coelom), Arthropoda (largest phylum, chitin), Mollusca (mantle, radula), Echinodermata (water vascular system; adult radial, larva bilateral), Hemichordata, Chordata."], ["Classes", "Cyclostomata (jawless, no scales; Petromyzon, Myxine); Chondrichthyes (cartilaginous, placoid scales, no air bladder; Scoliodon, Pristis, Trygon); Osteichthyes (bony, air bladder; Labeo, Catla, Hippocampus, Exocoetus); Reptilia (dry scaly skin; Chelone, Naja); Aves (feathers, pneumatic bones; Aptenodytes penguin); Mammalia (mammary glands; Ornithorhynchus lays eggs; Macropus pouch; Pteropus; Balaenoptera; Delphinus)."], ["Heart chambers", "fishes 2; amphibians 3; most reptiles 3 (crocodile 4); birds 4; mammals 4."], ["[T]", "For each animal group, learn one salient feature and two examples; NEET always asks one of the two."]], "B2": [["Root regions", "root cap, meristematic, elongation, maturation (root hairs) (26.102)."], ["Phyllotaxy", "alternate (China rose, mustard, sunflower), opposite (Calotropis, guava), whorled (Alstonia)."], ["Inflorescence", "racemose (unlimited, acropetal) vs cymose (limited, basipetal). Flower position: hypogynous (superior ovary: mustard, China rose, brinjal), perigynous (rose, plum, peach), epigynous (inferior ovary: guava, cucumber, sunflower)."], ["Placentation", "marginal (pea), axile (China rose, tomato, lemon), parietal (mustard, Argemone), free central (Dianthus, primrose), basal (sunflower, marigold)."], ["Families", "Malvaceae, Cruciferae/Brassicaceae, Leguminosae/Fabaceae, Compositae/Asteraceae, Gramineae/Poaceae are in the syllabus; NEET 2026 and Re-NEET also asked Solanaceae (26.133, R.132), so learn Solanaceae and Liliaceae too. Floral formulas: Solanaceae ⊕ ⚥ K(5) C(5) A5 G(2); Fabaceae % ⚥ K(5) C1+2+(2) A(9)+1 G1; Brassicaceae ⊕ ⚥ K2+2 C4 A2+4 G(2); Malvaceae ⊕ ⚥ K(5) C5 A(∞) G(5); Liliaceae ⊕ ⚥ P(3+3) A3+3 G(3)."], ["Seed", "dicot (bean: testa, tegmen, hilum, micropyle, 2 cotyledons, plumule, radicle); monocot (maize: scutellum = single cotyledon, coleoptile, coleorhiza, aleurone layer, endosperm). Non-albuminous seeds (pea, groundnut, bean) have no residual endosperm (R.145)."], ["Tissues", "meristematic (apical, intercalary, lateral); simple permanent (parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma); complex (xylem, phloem). Dicot stem: vascular bundles in a ring, conjoint, collateral, open, hypodermis collenchymatous; monocot stem: scattered, closed, sclerenchymatous hypodermis, bundle sheath (25.118). Dorsiventral leaf (palisade + spongy) vs isobilateral (monocot, bulliform cells; guard cells dumb-bell in grasses, kidney in dicots) (24.111, 26.107)."], ["Animal tissues", "simple squamous (alveoli, blood vessels), cuboidal (PCT with brush border, gland ducts), columnar (stomach, intestine), ciliated (bronchioles, fallopian tubes), compound (skin). Muscle: skeletal (striated, voluntary, multinucleate), smooth (spindle, involuntary, no striations), cardiac (striated, involuntary, intercalated discs). Junctions: tight, adhering, gap."], ["Frog (Rana tigrina)", "moist skin (cutaneous respiration); 3-chambered heart (2 atria, 1 ventricle) with sinus venosus and truncus arteriosus; renal portal and hepatic portal systems; nucleated RBCs; kidneys mesonephric, ureotelic; 10 pairs of cranial nerves; male has vocal sacs and a copulatory pad on the first digit of the forelimb; external fertilisation."], ["Cockroach (Periplaneta americana)", "— the official text prints \"insect (Frog)\", so confirm with your teacher; NEET asked cockroach in 2019–2024 (24.172, 24.193): 10 abdominal segments; forewings = tegmina (mesothorax), hindwings membranous; anal cerci in both sexes, anal styles only in males; crop, gizzard, hepatic caecae, Malpighian tubules; open circulation with 13-chambered heart; tracheal respiration through 10 pairs of spiracles; nervous system 3 thoracic and 6 abdominal ganglia on a ventral nerve cord."], ["[T]", "Floral formula: write symbol-by-symbol: ⊕ regular, % zygomorphic, ⚥ bisexual; G̲ superior, Ḡ inferior ovary."]], "B3": [["Prokaryotes", "70S ribosomes (50S + 30S), nucleoid, plasmid, mesosome, no membrane-bound organelles. Eukaryotes: 80S (60S + 40S); mitochondria and chloroplasts have 70S ribosomes and circular DNA (24.139)."], ["Nucleolus", "rRNA synthesis and ribosome assembly (26.92). Golgi: packaging, glycosylation, secretion. Lysosomes: hydrolytic enzymes, acidic pH. RER: protein synthesis; SER: lipids, steroids, detoxification."], ["Chloroplast", "grana (light reaction), stroma (dark reaction). Mitochondria: matrix (TCA enzymes), inner membrane (ETS, F₀F₁). Plastids: chromoplast (carotenoids, xanthophyll), leucoplast (amyloplast, elaioplast, aleuroplast)."], ["Cell membrane", "fluid mosaic model (Singer and Nicolson 1972); cell theory: Schleiden and Schwann; Virchow \"omnis cellula e cellula\"."], ["Biomolecules", "phospholipid lecithin (phosphatidylcholine); protein levels primary (peptide bond), secondary (α-helix, β-sheet), tertiary, quaternary (haemoglobin α₂β₂); collagen is the most abundant animal protein; RuBisCO the most abundant on Earth. Polysaccharides: starch (helical), cellulose (no helical structure), glycogen, chitin (N-acetylglucosamine). Nucleotide = base + sugar + phosphate; bonds: N-glycosidic (base–sugar), phosphoester (sugar–phosphate), phosphodiester (between nucleotides)."], ["Enzymes", "six classes in order: oxidoreductases, transferases, hydrolases, lyases, isomerases, ligases. [T] \"Only The Hungry Lion Is Lazy\". Apoenzyme + cofactor = holoenzyme; prosthetic group (haem in catalase), coenzyme (NAD, NADP, vitamin derivatives), metal ion. Competitive inhibitor resembles the substrate (Km rises, Vmax unchanged), e.g. malonate vs succinate dehydrogenase. Km = substrate concentration at ½Vmax. Low temperature inactivates reversibly; high temperature denatures."], ["Secondary metabolites", "pigments (carotenoids, anthocyanins), alkaloids (morphine, codeine), terpenoids, essential oils, toxins (abrin, ricin), lectins (concanavalin A), drugs (vinblastine, curcumin), polymeric substances (rubber, gums)."], ["Cell cycle", "G₁, S (DNA replication, 2C → 4C), G₂, M; G₀ quiescent. Mitosis: prophase (condensation), metaphase (chromosomes at the equator, spindle attached to kinetochores), anaphase (centromere splits), telophase; cytokinesis by furrow (animals) or cell plate (plants)."], ["Meiosis I prophase I", "leptotene, zygotene (synapsis, synaptonemal complex), pachytene (crossing over), diplotene (chiasmata; oocytes arrest here), diakinesis (terminalisation, nucleolus disappears). [T] \"Lazy Zebras Prefer Dull Days\". Meiosis I is reductional, II equational."], ["[T]", "For every organelle, one line: structure, function, one disease or one unusual fact."]], "B4": [["Photosynthesis", "O₂ comes from water (van Niel; Ruben isotope); chlorophyll a is the main pigment; chromatogram from top: carotene (yellow-orange, travels farthest), xanthophyll (yellow), chlorophyll a (blue-green), chlorophyll b (yellow-green) (25.122)."], ["Light reaction", "PSII (P680, grana) photolysis of water in the lumen; PSI (P700, stroma lamellae). Non-cyclic gives ATP, NADPH, O₂; cyclic (PSI only) gives ATP only. Chemiosmosis: proton gradient across the thylakoid, ATP synthase CF₀–CF₁, protons accumulate in the lumen."], ["Calvin cycle (stroma)", "carboxylation (RuBisCO: RuBP + CO₂ → 2 PGA), reduction, regeneration. Per CO₂: 3 ATP + 2 NADPH; per glucose 18 ATP + 12 NADPH, 6 turns. C₄ pathway: Kranz anatomy, PEP carboxylase in mesophyll → OAA → bundle sheath CO₂; no photorespiration, higher temperature optimum (C₄ 30–40 °C, C₃ 20–25 °C); maize, sugarcane, sorghum."], ["Photorespiration", "RuBisCO oxygenase; chloroplast → peroxisome → mitochondrion; no ATP/NADPH made; loses CO₂ (R.93)."], ["Respiration", "glycolysis in cytoplasm (net 2 ATP + 2 NADH, 2 pyruvate); fermentation (yeast: ethanol + CO₂; muscle: lactic acid); TCA in matrix: per turn 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 GTP/ATP, 2 CO₂; succinate dehydrogenase is Complex II on the inner membrane (25.91). ETS: Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase), II (SDH), III (cyt bc₁), IV (cytochrome c oxidase), O₂ final acceptor; NCERT counts 38 ATP per glucose (aerobic)."], ["Growth", "phases meristematic, elongation, maturation; arithmetic vs geometric growth (R.121); differentiation, dedifferentiation (cambium), redifferentiation; plasticity (heterophylly in cotton, coriander, larkspur) (26.111)."], ["PGRs", "Auxin (IAA, IBA, NAA; 2,4-D herbicide for dicots; apical dominance, root initiation, parthenocarpy in tomato, prevents leaf/fruit drop); Gibberellin (internode elongation, bolting, sugarcane yield, seedless fruit, malting, conifer maturity); Cytokinin (cell division, zeatin, delays senescence, breaks apical dominance); Ethylene (gas; fruit ripening, female flowers in cucumber, rapid internode growth in deep-water rice, breaks dormancy); ABA (stress hormone; closes stomata, seed dormancy; antagonist of GA)."], ["[T]", "PGR match questions: one word each: auxin = apical dominance/herbicide, GA = stem, cytokinin = divide, ethylene = ripen, ABA = stress."]], "B5": [["Lung volumes (mL)", "TV 500; IRV 2500–3000; ERV 1000–1100; RV 1100–1200; IC = TV + IRV; EC = TV + ERV; FRC = ERV + RV; VC = ERV + TV + IRV; TLC = VC + RV (~5800). Ventilation = TV × rate."], ["Gas transport", "CO₂ ~70% as bicarbonate (carbonic anhydrase in RBC), 20–25% carbamino-haemoglobin, ~7% dissolved; O₂ ~97% on Hb; Hb binds 4 O₂. Sigmoid dissociation curve shifts right (less affinity) with ↑pCO₂, ↑H⁺ (↓pH), ↑temperature. Alveolar pO₂ 104, pCO₂ 40 mmHg. Rhythm centre in the medulla; pneumotaxic centre in the pons; chemosensitive area next to the rhythm centre senses CO₂ and H⁺."], ["Disorders", "asthma (bronchial inflammation), emphysema (damaged alveolar walls, smoking), occupational (silicosis, asbestosis)."], ["Blood", "plasma 55%; RBC 5–5.5 million/mm³ (no nucleus in mammals, ~120 days, spleen \"graveyard\"); WBC 6000–8000/mm³: neutrophils 60–65% (most abundant, phagocytic), lymphocytes 20–25%, monocytes 6–8% (largest), eosinophils 2–3%, basophils 0.5–1% (histamine, heparin). [T] \"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\". Platelets 1.5–3.5 lakh/mm³. Clotting: thromboplastin → prothrombin → thrombin (needs Ca²⁺) → fibrinogen → fibrin threads (R.115)."], ["Heart", "SAN (pacemaker) 70–75 per minute → AVN → bundle of His → Purkinje fibres; cycle 0.8 s (atrial systole 0.1, ventricular systole 0.3, joint diastole 0.4); lub = closure of AV valves, dub = closure of semilunar valves; cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate (≈ 70 mL × 72 ≈ 5 L/min). ECG: P = atrial depolarisation; QRS = ventricular depolarisation (start of systole); T = ventricular repolarisation (end of systole). Sympathetic ↑ rate; parasympathetic ↓. Hypertension ≥ 140/90; angina = chest pain from poor O₂ supply; heart failure = heart not pumping effectively."], ["Excretion", "ammonotelic (bony fish, aquatic amphibians), ureotelic (mammals, terrestrial amphibians), uricotelic (birds, reptiles, insects). Nephron: PCT (brush border, reabsorbs nearly all glucose and amino acids, 70–80% of water and electrolytes), loop of Henle (descending permeable to water, ascending impermeable to water), DCT (conditional reabsorption, secretes H⁺, K⁺, NH₃), collecting duct (water reabsorption by ADH). Medullary osmolarity rises from 300 to 1200 mOsm/L. Juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%, long loop, vasa recta) concentrate urine (24.187). GFR 125 mL/min. JGA (JG cells + macula densa) → renin → angiotensinogen → angiotensin I → II (vasoconstriction ↑GFR) → aldosterone (Na⁺, water reabsorption); ANF from atria opposes this (26.145). ADH from the neurohypophysis; deficiency causes diabetes insipidus. Disorders: uraemia, renal calculi, glomerulonephritis; dialysis (cellophane, heparin)."], ["Locomotion", "sarcomere Z to Z; A band (myosin, constant), I band (actin, shortens), H zone (myosin only, disappears). Contraction: ACh at the neuromuscular junction → Ca²⁺ from sarcoplasmic reticulum → binds troponin → tropomyosin moves → myosin head cross-bridges to actin, using ATP. Red fibres (myoglobin, aerobic) vs white fibres (anaerobic). Disorders: myasthenia gravis (autoimmune, neuromuscular junction), muscular dystrophy (genetic), tetany (low Ca²⁺), arthritis, osteoporosis (↓ bone mass, ↓ oestrogen), gout (uric acid crystals)."], ["Skeleton", "206 bones; axial 80, appendicular 126; vertebral column 26 vertebrae (cervical 7, thoracic 12, lumbar 5, sacrum 1 (fused 5), coccyx 1 (fused 4)); ribs 7 true, 3 false, 2 floating pairs. Joints: fibrous (skull sutures), cartilaginous (vertebrae), synovial: ball-and-socket (shoulder, hip), hinge (knee, elbow), pivot (atlas–axis), gliding (carpals), saddle (thumb)."], ["Neural", "resting potential from K⁺ leak and the Na⁺/K⁺ pump (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in); action potential: Na⁺ influx then K⁺ efflux; synapses electrical (rare, gap junction) or chemical (neurotransmitter, ACh). Forebrain: cerebrum (hemispheres joined by corpus callosum), thalamus (relay), hypothalamus (temperature, hunger, thirst, hormones); midbrain (corpora quadrigemina); hindbrain: pons, cerebellum (balance), medulla (heart rate, respiration). Meninges: dura (outer), arachnoid, pia (inner)."], ["Endocrine", "hypothalamus: releasing (GnRH, TRH, CRH, GHRH) and inhibiting (somatostatin, dopamine) hormones via the hypophyseal portal; anterior pituitary: GH (acromegaly in adults, gigantism in children, dwarfism if deficient), prolactin, TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH; posterior: oxytocin, vasopressin. Pineal: melatonin. Thyroid: T₃/T₄ (goitre = iodine deficiency; exophthalmic goitre = hyper; cretinism), thyrocalcitonin ↓ Ca²⁺. Parathyroid: PTH ↑ Ca²⁺. Thymus: thymosin. Adrenal cortex: cortisol (gluconeogenesis, anti-inflammatory), aldosterone; Addison's disease (cortex deficiency); medulla: adrenaline and noradrenaline (\"emergency\" hormones: ↑ alertness, pupil dilation, sweating, heartbeat, glycogenolysis). Pancreas: glucagon (α, hyperglycaemic), insulin (β, hypoglycaemic, GLUT-4), somatostatin (δ); diabetes mellitus. Testis: androgens (Leydig); ovary: oestrogen, progesterone. Peptide hormones act via membrane receptors and second messengers (cAMP, IP₃-Ca²⁺); steroid and thyroid hormones act on intracellular receptors and gene expression (24.174)."], ["[T]", "Hormone matching: write the gland once, then list \"excess\" and \"deficiency\" disease."]], "B6": [["Flowering plants", "anther is tetragonal with 4 microsporangia; wall layers: epidermis, endothecium, middle layers, tapetum (innermost, nourishes pollen) (25.153). Pollen: exine (sporopollenin) and intine. Embryo sac: 7 cells, 8 nuclei: 3 antipodals (chalazal), 2 synergids (micropylar, filiform apparatus), egg, central cell with 2 polar nuclei. Pollination: autogamy, geitonogamy, xenogamy (26.134). Wind pollination: light non-sticky pollen, feathery stigma, single ovule, many flowers (maize). Water pollination is rare (Vallisneria, Hydrilla; most water plants are pollinated by insects or wind) (24.104). Double fertilisation: syngamy → zygote (2n); triple fusion → PEN (3n) (26.122, R.171). Ploidy: pollen n; synergid n; antipodal n; nucellus 2n; MMC 2n; endosperm 3n. Apomixis (Asteraceae, grasses); polyembryony (citrus, mango); parthenocarpy (banana)."], ["Human male", "testes in scrotum (2–2.5 °C lower); seminiferous tubules (Sertoli cells nourish; Leydig cells outside produce androgens); path: rete testis → vasa efferentia → epididymis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct → urethra; seminal vesicles (fructose, ~60% of semen), prostate (alkaline), bulbourethral (lubricating mucus). Spermatogenesis: spermatogonia (2n) → primary spermatocyte (2n) → secondary spermatocytes (n) → spermatids → sperms. LH acts on Leydig cells (androgens); FSH acts on Sertoli cells (spermiogenesis) (24.188). Sperm: acrosome (enzymes), nucleus, middle piece (mitochondria), tail (25.110)."], ["Human female", "fallopian tube: infundibulum (fimbriae) → ampulla (fertilisation) → isthmus; uterus layers perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium. Hymen: its presence or absence is not a reliable virginity indicator (24.168). Oogenesis: oogonia form only in foetal life; primary oocytes arrest at prophase I until puberty; secondary oocyte (n) arrested at metaphase II and completes meiosis II only after sperm entry (second polar body)."], ["Menstrual cycle (28 days)", "menstrual (1–5), follicular (6–13; FSH and oestrogen ↑), ovulation (day 14; LH surge), luteal (15–28; corpus luteum, progesterone). Fertilisation → cleavage → morula → blastocyst (trophoblast + inner cell mass) → implantation. Placenta hormones: hCG, hPL, oestrogens, progesterone, relaxin. Foetus: heart forms in the first month; limbs and digits by 8 weeks; first movements and hair on head in the 5th month; eyelashes by 24 weeks (26.141). Parturition: foetal ejection reflex and oxytocin. Colostrum: first milk, rich in IgA (24.183)."], ["Reproductive health", "barriers (condoms, diaphragms, caps); IUDs (non-medicated Lippes loop; copper-releasing CuT, Cu7, Multiload 375 reduce sperm motility; hormone-releasing Progestasert, LNG-20 make the uterus unsuitable for implantation); natural methods (periodic abstinence days 10–17, withdrawal, lactational amenorrhoea up to ~6 months); pills (21-day oestrogen–progestogen; Saheli weekly, non-steroidal); surgical (vasectomy, tubectomy); MTP in the first 12 weeks is safest; amniocentesis (chromosomal disorders; sex determination for misuse is banned). STDs: gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes, chlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis B, HIV. ART: IVF (zygote or ≤ 8 blastomeres into the fallopian tube = ZIFT; more than 8 into the uterus = IUT), GIFT (ovum from a donor into the fallopian tube), ICSI (sperm injected into the ovum) (26.138, R.137), AI/IUI."], ["[T]", "Draw the menstrual graph once: LH peak at day 14, progesterone peak at day 21."]], "B7": [["Mendel", "dominance, segregation, independent assortment; monohybrid F₂ 3:1 (genotype 1:2:1); dihybrid 9:3:3:1; test cross with recessive; incomplete dominance gives 1:2:1 in both phenotype and genotype (snapdragon, Mirabilis); co-dominance (ABO IᴬIᴮ; MN); multiple alleles (Iᴬ, Iᴮ, i; a person carries two); pleiotropy (phenylketonuria, sickle cell); polygenic (skin colour, height; 3 gene pairs → 7 phenotypes, ratio 1:6:15:20:15:6:1)."], ["ABO genotypes", "A = IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi; B = IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi; AB = IᴬIᴮ; O = ii."], ["Sex determination", "XX–XY (humans, Drosophila); XX–XO (grasshopper, male XO) (26.154); ZZ–ZW (birds, female heterogametic); honey bee haplodiploid: queen and workers 2n = 32, drones n = 16 (from unfertilised eggs) producing sperm by mitosis (26.118, 26.159)."], ["Disorders", "sex-linked recessive (haemophilia, red–green colour blindness); autosomal recessive (sickle cell: GAG → GTG in DNA, Glu → Val at position 6 of the β chain (GAG → GUG in mRNA) (26.137); thalassaemia: defective globin synthesis; phenylketonuria); chromosomal (Down's 21 trisomy 47; Klinefelter's XXY; Turner's XO, 45) (R.180)."], ["Molecular basis", "Griffith (transformation), Avery–MacLeod–McCarty (DNA is the transforming principle), Hershey–Chase (³²P DNA, ³⁵S protein) (25.134). DNA: antiparallel, 10 bp per turn, pitch 3.4 nm, 0.34 nm between base pairs; Chargaff A = T, G = C. Human haploid genome 3 × 10⁹ bp (~2.2 m of DNA); chromosome 1 has the most genes, Y the fewest. Histones are positively charged (lysine, arginine), forming an octamer (H2A, H2B, H3, H4 × 2) with ~200 bp per nucleosome (25.146, 26.123). Euchromatin is active; heterochromatin is dense and inactive."], ["Replication", "semi-conservative (Meselson–Stahl); DNA polymerase works 5′ → 3′; leading strand continuous, lagging strand discontinuous (Okazaki fragments, DNA ligase) (24.150)."], ["Transcription", "template strand 3′ → 5′; RNA matches the coding strand with U for T. Unit = promoter (upstream) + structural gene + terminator (24.110, 26.104). Bacterial RNA polymerase with σ factor (initiation) and ρ factor (termination) (25.174). Eukaryotes: Pol I (rRNAs), Pol II (hnRNA → mRNA), Pol III (tRNA, 5S rRNA, snRNA) (R.172). hnRNA processing: splicing, capping (methyl-guanosine at 5′), tailing (poly-A at 3′) (25.129)."], ["Genetic code", "triplet; 64 codons, 61 code amino acids, 3 stop (UAA, UAG, UGA); AUG start (Met), UGG only Trp; degenerate, non-overlapping, nearly universal (25.163). tRNA is the adapter (cloverleaf; L-shaped tertiary structure)."], ["Lac operon", "(Jacob and Monod): i gene codes the repressor; z = β-galactosidase; y = permease (increases permeability); a = transacetylase; lactose (allolactose) is the inducer, it binds the repressor and frees the operator; polycistronic mRNA (24.133, 26.125)."], ["DNA fingerprinting (Jeffreys)", "VNTR; steps: isolate DNA → restriction digest → gel electrophoresis → Southern blotting → hybridisation with labelled VNTR probe → autoradiography (26.116)."], ["Evolution", "Oparin–Haldane (chemical evolution); Miller–Urey 1953 (CH₄, H₂, NH₃, water vapour at 800 °C with electric discharge → amino acids). Evidence: fossils, comparative anatomy, biogeography, biochemistry. Homologous organs (divergent evolution): forelimbs of whale, bat, cheetah, human (R.162). Analogous organs (convergent): wings of butterfly and bird, eyes of octopus and mammals, potato (stem) vs sweet potato (root) (25.151)."], ["Darwin", "variation, struggle for existence, survival of the fittest; Lamarck: use and disuse; de Vries: mutation (saltation). Selection types: stabilising (average birth weight), directional (peppered moth, DDT or antibiotic resistance), disruptive (both extremes) (R.175). Adaptive radiation: Darwin's finches, Australian marsupials."], ["Hardy–Weinberg", "p² + 2pq + q² = 1; p + q = 1; equilibrium needs no mutation, no gene flow, no genetic drift, no recombination, no selection (24.179, R.159)."], ["Eras and humans", "Dryopithecus → Ramapithecus → Australopithecus (~2 mya) → Homo habilis (650–800 cc) → Homo erectus (900 cc) → Neanderthal (1400 cc) → Homo sapiens (arose in Africa) (24.157, 26.140, R.143). Mesozoic ended with dinosaur extinction; Cenozoic is the age of mammals (24.196)."], ["[T]", "Genetics: practise one pedigree problem every day for a week; the rest follows."]], "B8": [["Pathogens", "viruses (rhinovirus: common cold; dengue and chikungunya spread by *Aedes*; hepatitis B; HIV); bacteria (typhoid: *Salmonella typhi*, Widal test; pneumonia: *Streptococcus pneumoniae*); protozoa (malaria: *Plasmodium*, *P. falciparum* most dangerous; amoebiasis: *Entamoeba*); helminths (ascariasis: *Ascaris*; filariasis: *Wuchereria*, spread by *Culex*); fungi (ringworm: *Microsporum*, *Trichophyton*, *Epidermophyton*)."], ["Immunity", "innate (skin and mucus barriers, acidic stomach, tears; cells: neutrophils, monocytes, NK cells, macrophages; interferons) (25.167); acquired (primary slow, secondary strong, with memory); humoral (B cells, antibodies) vs cell-mediated (T cells, graft rejection). Antibody = 2 heavy + 2 light chains (H₂L₂); IgG most abundant; IgE in allergy; IgA in colostrum. Active immunity (vaccines) vs passive (readymade antibodies: antivenom, colostrum). Autoimmune: rheumatoid arthritis. Lymphoid organs: primary (bone marrow, thymus), secondary (spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, Peyer's patches, MALT, appendix)."], ["AIDS", "HIV is a retrovirus (RNA → DNA by reverse transcriptase) that infects helper T cells and macrophages; spread by sex, blood, needles, mother to child; ELISA test; not by touch or sharing food."], ["Cancer", "loss of contact inhibition; carcinogens: physical (X-rays, γ, UV), chemical (tobacco smoke), biological (oncogenic viruses; proto-oncogenes). Detection: biopsy, radiography, CT, MRI, antibodies against tumour antigens. Treatment: surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, immunotherapy (α-interferon)."], ["Drugs", "opioids — morphine from *Papaver somniferum* (sedative, painkiller), heroin = diacetylmorphine (depressant); cannabinoids from *Cannabis sativa* (marijuana, hashish, charas, ganja); cocaine from *Erythroxylum coca* (interferes with dopamine transport, stimulant); atropine (*Atropa belladonna*) and *Datura* are hallucinogens."], ["Microbes", "LAB (*Lactobacillus*) converts milk to curd; yeast (*Saccharomyces*) in bread and beer; Swiss cheese holes by *Propionibacterium sharmanii* (CO₂); Roquefort ripened by *Penicillium roqueforti*. Beverages without distillation: wine, beer; with distillation: whisky, brandy, rum (25.103). Acids: citric (*Aspergillus niger*), acetic (*Acetobacter aceti*), lactic (*Lactobacillus*), butyric (*Clostridium butylicum*). Streptokinase (*Streptococcus*) clot buster; cyclosporin A (*Trichoderma polysporum*) immunosuppressant; statins (*Monascus purpureus*) lower cholesterol. Penicillin: Fleming 1928, Chain and Florey; *Penicillium notatum*."], ["Sewage", "primary (physical sedimentation) then secondary (biological: aerobic flocs, BOD ↓); anaerobic digesters give biogas (methanogens, *Methanobacterium*: CH₄ + CO₂ + H₂ + H₂S). Biocontrol: *Bacillus thuringiensis*, *Trichoderma*, baculoviruses. Biofertilisers: *Rhizobium* (legume nodules), *Azospirillum* and *Azotobacter* (free-living), *Anabaena* (with *Azolla*), *Nostoc*, mycorrhiza (*Glomus*: phosphorus uptake, drought resistance) (25.113)."], ["[T]", "Make a table: disease | pathogen | vector or route. Revise weekly."]], "B9": [["pBR322", "ori, *amp*ᴿ, *tet*ᴿ, *rop*; BamHI/SalI sit in *tet*ᴿ; PstI/PvuI in *amp*ᴿ. Insertional inactivation: foreign DNA at BamHI inactivates *tet*ᴿ, so recombinants are ampicillin-resistant and tetracycline-sensitive (26.136). Blue–white selection (lacZ): recombinants are white (25.179)."], ["DNA isolation", "lysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plants), chitinase (fungi), RNase, protease; DNA precipitates as threads in chilled ethanol (R.136)."], ["Gel electrophoresis", "DNA is negatively charged and moves to the anode; smaller fragments move farther; stained with ethidium bromide and seen under UV (orange) (25.136, 26.120)."], ["Host entry", "Ca²⁺ and heat shock (42 °C) for bacteria; micro-injection (animal cells); gene gun with gold or tungsten particles (plants); disarmed *Agrobacterium tumefaciens* Ti plasmid (24.153)."], ["Bioreactor", "stirred tank with sparger (oxygen), foam breaker, temperature and pH control (25.126)."], ["RNAi", "dsRNA silences mRNA; used against *Meloidogyne incognita* (nematode) in tobacco (25.144)."], ["Insulin", "A chain (21 aa) and B chain (30 aa) joined by disulphide bonds; pro-insulin has a C peptide removed on maturation; Eli Lilly (1983) made the chains separately in *E. coli*; oral insulin is digested (25.116, 25.161)."], ["Gene therapy", "ADA deficiency (first case 1990; lymphocytes given a functional ADA gene by a retroviral vector; not a permanent cure). Molecular diagnosis: PCR, ELISA, probes. Transgenic animals: Rosie (cow, human α-lactalbumin enriched milk); α-1-antitrypsin for emphysema (26.162). Biosafety: GEAC. Biopiracy (basmati, turmeric, neem), bioprospecting (26.117)."], ["[T]", "Draw pBR322 once with all restriction sites and mark which gene each cut site sits in."]], "B10": [["Population growth", "exponential (J-curve, dN/dt = rN); logistic (Verhulst–Pearl, dN/dt = rN(K − N)/K, sigmoid, K = carrying capacity); r = b − d (24.116, 25.180)."], ["Interactions", "mutualism (+,+: lichens, mycorrhiza, fig–wasp, orchid *Ophrys*–bee sexual deceit); competition (−,−: Gause's competitive exclusion; Abingdon tortoise vs goats; resource partitioning, MacArthur's warblers); predation (+,−; keystone predator *Pisaster*; prickly pear and *Cactoblastis*); parasitism (+,−; brood parasitism cuckoo–crow); commensalism (+,0: orchid on a mango branch (epiphyte), barnacles on a whale, cattle egret); amensalism (0,−: *Penicillium*)."], ["Ecosystem", "NPP = GPP − R; only ~50% of incident solar radiation is PAR and 2–10% of PAR is captured; secondary productivity is formed by consumers. Units: g m⁻² yr⁻¹ or kcal m⁻² yr⁻¹. Decomposition: fragmentation → leaching → catabolism → humification → mineralisation; lignin and chitin slow it, sugars and nitrogen speed it. Energy flow 10% law (Lindeman). Grazing food chain dominates aquatic ecosystems; detritus food chain dominates terrestrial. Pyramids: number (can be inverted: tree–insect–bird), biomass (inverted in the sea), energy (always upright) (26.149)."], ["Biodiversity", "three levels (genetic, species, ecosystem); India has 2.4% of land area and 8.1% of world species; species–area relationship log S = log C + Z log A (Z 0.1–0.2 for small areas, 0.6–1.2 for very large areas); latitudinal gradient (more species near the equator); Amazon rain forest: 40,000 plant species, 3000 fishes, 1300 birds. Robert May's estimate: ~7 million species. Rivet-popper hypothesis (Paul Ehrlich)."], ["Evil quartet", "habitat loss and fragmentation (the most important), over-exploitation, alien species invasions (Nile perch, *Lantana*, water hyacinth, *Parthenium*), co-extinctions (25.164). Recent extinctions: dodo, quagga, thylacine, Steller's sea cow, three tiger subspecies."], ["Conservation", "in situ — 34 hotspots worldwide, 3 in India (Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma, Himalaya), biosphere reserves, national parks, sanctuaries, sacred groves; ex situ — zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, cryopreservation (24.108, 25.99, 26.124)."], ["Ramdeo Misra", "father of Indian ecology (25.133)."], ["[T]", "Ecology numbers (hotspots, percentages, species counts) are free marks; write them on one flash card."]]};